b92ba7b8-27c3-402e-a0e5-0f219c630d6c	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777713800.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777713800.000000", "text": ":octo_alert: Re the \\"go faster\\" request from Frank.\\n\\nI would like to send an official response by cob tomorrow - please review the summary created here from the slack answers and the call - i need to re-word to make them customer facing.  Add anything else you may have thought of please to the document rather than here and comment as appropriate.  Ill action tomorrow\\n\\nThanks", "type": "message", "user": "U_ANNE_7F9C7274"}]	{U_ANNE_7F9C7274}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.219134+00	2026-05-27 01:58:08.800063+00	summarized	2026-05-26
9bd4656e-0aa4-41cf-843d-5c8448ed4141	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777713900.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777713900.000000", "text": "@Philip Jones @James Force im curious, for one release of OCP how many images are actually synced in this environment?\\nAlso, how many images have we synced in total with the different versions? (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_ALEXAN_D03599F6"}]	{U_ALEXAN_D03599F6}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.223678+00	2026-05-27 02:00:10.18806+00	summarized	2026-05-26
265cb088-8c86-4132-8be9-54b4cfa1d346	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777714000.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777714000.000000", "text": "Build Update\\n\\nDELL CSM Updated on 104\\nNow includes OTEL metrics, credit to @ricardo gregorio \\nMeeting to be scheduled by Dell for next week to discuss operator improvements.\\n\\nLatest EntraID issue identified and fix is pending a proxy change for cluster 301. \\nPortworx operator upgrade prepared. We are just waiting on some required images to mirror now.", "type": "message", "user": "U_PHILIP_740C6823"}]	{U_PHILIP_740C6823}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.227421+00	2026-05-27 02:08:08.466392+00	summarized	2026-05-26
6d275b1e-32e4-4067-a712-c20bdf775566	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777714100.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777714100.000000", "text": "@Rydekull @Shebi\\nSo regarding Sudhirs request on the failed migration of Windows Failover Cluster we made some progress over the day:\\nProblem 1 solved: The migration itself is now successfull with all PVCs being available to both VMs. :check:\\nMulti rooted issues, the migration plan execution order and configuration. Also the disks have been there once and then recreated as copy-[pvc-name..] which led to the VMs had different set of disks mounted.\\nProblem 2 solved: The VMs do boot up now successfully after the Migration. :check:\\nIssue was wrong NAD mappings and one NAD missing in the destination namespace.\\n\\nNew problem: The Windows Failover Cluster itself fails, I have some experience with Windows Server from 10+ years ago, nevertheless I wondered where do we draw the line in supporting applications after the migration.\\nAnyway, when trying to bring the disks online it just says device i/o error, the Windows OS does see the disks but can not communicate... totally sounds like the good old SCSI3-PR issue has another place in the spotlight... ?Arpit Tolani  [6:34 PM]\\nI was pulled into a call regarding Microsoft Entra ID authentication failing on one cluster. On that cluster, the customer had a production proxy configured, and authentication was not working.\\nThe same Entra ID setup was configured on another cluster using a different proxy, where authentication was working as expected.\\nDuring debugging, I found that the non-working cluster was unable to reach Microsoft Graph (graph.microsoft.com), which was being blocked by the proxy.\\nAs a temporary workaround, I suggested allowing access to that endpoint. I also advised the customer to open a support case with Microsoft or obtain an official list of required URLs. Since dependencies may change (for example, due to outages or load balancing across different endpoints), relying on a single allowed domain may not be sufficient—it's better to follow Microsoft’s documented and supported endpoint list.Carina Almeida  [7:10 PM]\\nAnd why are you working at this time @atolani? 'go home' and speak to you tomorrow :wink:", "type": "message", "user": "U_JENS_9E9831E8"}]	{U_JENS_9E9831E8}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.231588+00	2026-05-27 02:11:00.252169+00	summarized	2026-05-26
36ace05a-c763-4bb1-9e40-cdc1eea18e85	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777714200.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777714200.000000", "text": "Hi! I am going to send this to ACN as they requested it in one of the daily calls. In case anyone wants to review it before I send it. It doesn't say anything that they don't know. https://docs.google.com/document/d/1N9gfWsoON2swOG0oijO62EzQs00cwzZxkF1Ke8ohG3o/edit?tab=t.x4ic3bxehg1n", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}]	{U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.248059+00	2026-05-27 02:12:45.486091+00	summarized	2026-05-26
23dcc11b-f398-4818-b8e1-07a869dcf450	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777714300.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777714300.000000", "text": "Hi all - Are we involved in any of the discussions here?\\n\\non the risk call today there is concern that the demise of the source VM hardware and a rebuild as a OpenShift is a risk to the velocity of the migrations as 1 this hasnt been tested and 2 the demise process isnt fully cordinated.  @James Force - is there any discussion in the build stream about these rebuilds? (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_ANNE_7F9C7274"}]	{U_ANNE_7F9C7274}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.257137+00	2026-05-27 02:14:24.974851+00	summarized	2026-05-26
21588eb3-12a6-4193-a29f-3012bbc5b242	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777714400.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777714400.000000", "text": "Hi @pdelashe, just reviewing the open support cases....can proactive case https://access.redhat.com/support/cases/#/case/04414181 be closed now? If, so, are you able to close it as you raised it IIRC?", "type": "message", "user": "U_NICK_CA725671"}]	{U_NICK_CA725671}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.269373+00	2026-05-27 02:16:10.584482+00	summarized	2026-05-26
3a2ae747-2657-4e4c-a7a7-3b9d03e7c75c	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777713400.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777713400.000000", "text": "[security - network policies] \\n@Rydekull one for next week...(FYI i'm not actuallly working today, just so happen to have a bit of overlap with some uni work of mine... hence the question :wink:)\\n\\nAs for the workload networkpolicy he wants setting up is that just a deny all basically? - I was thinking... even tho we're using CUDN we still need to account for live migration and maybe namespace to namespace migration right (forward looking a bit)? as those comms would be over the pod network right? I think I'll maybe just need to test this in a lab, but curious if you have any pointers here. How deny all are we going for here? Can you remind me on the logic of what he wants to do here and why (if its more than just because) - something re system level workloads right? I was just thinking of what we need to consider here if anything at all\\n\\nI assume Natti is aware of this yeah? (i'm sure we've talked on this before) https://access.redhat.com/articles/7133113 + https://docs.google.com/document/d/1m1ne1z4iBfB66d30-HXUN9-FDBGtXN75asVe5oyWxiA/edit?usp=sharing just wanted to check on that too\\nRed Hat Customer PortalOperators shipping with network policies may require OCP cluster upgrade before they can be upgraded - Red Hat Customer PortalBeginning February 2027, each Operator from Red Hat is required to ship with Network Policies. This will require to update your cluster to a new minimum versions, otherwise these updates will fail to apply and manual intervention is required.Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga  [5:03 PM]\\n[Migration Update]\\nThe Situarday with Dell, there was a clear directive from Narender to keep all VMs residing on Dell storage out of today’s migration batches until further notice. Unfortunately, there was a breakdown in coordination with Accenture, and they were unable to triage and filter the Dell VMs out of the environment. As a result, we spent the whole day in firefighting mode.\\n\\nWe used the failed runs to gather additional logs from the system. These logs have been handed over to Dell so they can finally provide a permanent fix for the storage allocation.\\n\\nThe Good News (XFS Resolved!):\\nWe successfully pushed the fix for the XFS repair :party_cat: The few RHEL migrations that did succeed today (on non-Dell storage) proved that the XFS workaround is fully functional. This clears one of the blockers for our RHEL 7 workloads moving forward.\\n\\nNext Steps & Tuesday Resumption:\\nGiven the high volume of Dell-related failures, Vinay made the right call to cancel the remaining migrations for today. We are going to regroup and resume migrations on Tuesday (Hoping ACN does a good triage).\\n\\nMigration Statistics (May 22, 2026)\\nWe had roughly 122 VMs in scope today across the batches.\\n    :large_green_circle: Success (Clean & Fix/Retry): 9 VMs\\n    :red_circle: Rollbacks: 59 VMs (Almost exclusively Dell Storage / LUN Mapping / Allocate Disk failures)\\n    :white_circle: Cancelled: 54 VMs (Cancelled by Vinay’s directive to halt the Dell batches)\\n\\nHave a great weekend everyone, and we will pick this back up on Tuesday!", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179"}]	{U_JAMES_F3856179}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.189505+00	2026-05-27 01:50:10.257852+00	summarized	2026-05-26
cfce0b52-e429-4c95-b1cc-8c7032020374	T_EOS_MVP	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	7777713500.000000	\N	1	[{"ts": "7777713500.000000", "text": "[Some PS IDs for t he new joiners]\\nMessage proj-eos-mvp", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}]	{U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB}	2026-05-25 11:51:53.195157+00	2026-05-27 01:51:45.586724+00	summarized	2026-05-26
