ce35ac1b-34cf-4866-8415-cc0e20927050	T01EOS001	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	1778220285.000225	\N	1	[{"ts": "1778220285.000225", "text": "[Due to their internal network setup missing firewalls...] We're being asked if we can prevent who can talk to the nodes and the kube api. Many ways to skin a cat. Stupid that it is required and not solved with a traditional firewall.\\nAny thoughts?", "type": "message", "user": "Alexander Rydekull"}]	{"Alexander Rydekull"}	2026-05-08 17:54:25.2772+00	2026-05-09 17:53:00.418764+00	pending_retry	2026-05-09
d8119474-0406-4ff2-a628-21be7123fab6	T01EOS001	153c4abb-b8f9-4e4b-8be0-c6e189ffd9b0	1778228507.000227	\N	1	[{"ts": "1778228507.000227", "text": "[build]\\nWe have restored cluster 104 this morning. It's now at least as good as it was before the storage update :wink:\\nCluster 102 pure storage update now successfully completed after we ran a cleanup script to remove stale entries in the pure storage cluster kvdb.\\nTake away from this is that the storage cluster had a lot of volume snapshots it thought it was managing that didn't actually exist on the flasharray. This was likely triggered by stress testing back in Feb.\\nWe still see a LOT of volume snapshots associated with non-existent, previously deleted pvc's. These are for the most case the same stale volumes the script removed from kvdb. We will need to create our own script to remove them and complete the cleanup.\\nIssues impacting hkoclu307 and the three nodes that refused to register now tracked to a Cisco switch issue. A ticket is being raised with Cisco to debug.\\nIsovalent call postponed for today.", "type": "message", "user": "Philip Jones"}]	{"Philip Jones"}	2026-05-08 17:54:25.293721+00	2026-05-09 17:59:00.155788+00	pending_retry	2026-05-09
