73f31591-9ea9-41e7-a2c9-3d85f926f3c6	fccaa7d9-514e-44ac-adb3-1f40e6ee74a1	1778229480.000000	Raffaele Spazzoli	[bsod] bsod as in blue scree of death for windows. This is apparently now affecting several of our customers. Some regression in either our product or windows. A customer pulled out of openshift virtualization for this. Engineering trying to figure out what is it.	{"ts": "1778229480.000000", "text": "[bsod] bsod as in blue scree of death for windows. This is apparently now affecting several of our customers. Some regression in either our product or windows. A customer pulled out of openshift virtualization for this. Engineering trying to figure out what is it.", "type": "message", "user": "Raffaele Spazzoli"}
66b45b1e-2f93-47c8-9798-af8445ae64d0	a7b5f218-c456-4884-aeef-15d6399918f9	1778230801.000001	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Sync on Engineering Prio CAP-978 EOSBank” meeting]: Overall, it was a productive session, and we identified a few key areas where we need to refine our approach:\nPriority & Expectation Alignment: Raffaele and I noticed a lack of synchronization between the bank’s priorities/impact and our own. Moving forward, we need to make a conscious effort to ensure our internal priorities are synced with the customer’s expectations so we are always pushing on the right levers.\nTAM Integration & Architectural Context: The TAMs are a fantastic addition to the team! However, to maximize their impact, we need to fast-track their onboarding. We have established a working dynamic, but it needs refinement. Specifically, we need to proactively provide the TAMs with much deeper architectural context so they can drive more value for the project. @Carina Almeida this is for the call we need for next week, most likely.\nRFE & Support Case Rhythm: Stepping back to analyze our Support Cases and RFEs in this format was incredibly useful to see what is truly important and what might be missing from our radar. We agreed that we need to establish a regular cadence for these alignment meetings. @Nikos @rspazzol add something if I missed	{"ts": "1778230801.000001", "text": "[Sync on Engineering Prio CAP-978 EOSBank” meeting]: Overall, it was a productive session, and we identified a few key areas where we need to refine our approach:\\nPriority & Expectation Alignment: Raffaele and I noticed a lack of synchronization between the bank’s priorities/impact and our own. Moving forward, we need to make a conscious effort to ensure our internal priorities are synced with the customer’s expectations so we are always pushing on the right levers.\\nTAM Integration & Architectural Context: The TAMs are a fantastic addition to the team! However, to maximize their impact, we need to fast-track their onboarding. We have established a working dynamic, but it needs refinement. Specifically, we need to proactively provide the TAMs with much deeper architectural context so they can drive more value for the project. @Carina Almeida this is for the call we need for next week, most likely.\\nRFE & Support Case Rhythm: Stepping back to analyze our Support Cases and RFEs in this format was incredibly useful to see what is truly important and what might be missing from our radar. We agreed that we need to establish a regular cadence for these alignment meetings. @Nikos @rspazzol add something if I missed", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
e42592b6-57a1-4dab-9fc9-25fee569c0f0	8acb0426-c368-4021-ab56-89939fa588ec	1778219582.000002	Raffaele Spazzoli	[michele meetings] We have everyday meetings with Michele, who is getting up to speed with all of the architecture design/decisions taken in the last few months. Not very interesting meetings so far. The only real meat in the discussion is how many ACM clusters should we have and where. I think we are moving towards the idea of having as few as possible of these cluster (maybe 2 UK and HCK). There is also a vague idea of using these ACM clusters to consolidate in teh future some other infra functions which have the same granularity, namely the rpm cache server and the AAP instances. Licence issues aside this seems like a good idea to me. As you probably remember one of our design principles is that if ACM dies, the workload clusters should keep working normally. For the consultant/architects, if you see any ACM policy that invalidates our principle, please let me know. This approach reduces the responsibility of ACM to: cluster installation (including gitops kickstart), centralized VM management tool (optional). In general the responsibilities are so limited that one can question the value of having ACM (I certainly am doing that). Michele would also like to activate the centralized observability capability.	{"ts": "1778219582.000002", "text": "[michele meetings] We have everyday meetings with Michele, who is getting up to speed with all of the architecture design/decisions taken in the last few months. Not very interesting meetings so far. The only real meat in the discussion is how many ACM clusters should we have and where. I think we are moving towards the idea of having as few as possible of these cluster (maybe 2 UK and HCK). There is also a vague idea of using these ACM clusters to consolidate in teh future some other infra functions which have the same granularity, namely the rpm cache server and the AAP instances. Licence issues aside this seems like a good idea to me. As you probably remember one of our design principles is that if ACM dies, the workload clusters should keep working normally. For the consultant/architects, if you see any ACM policy that invalidates our principle, please let me know. This approach reduces the responsibility of ACM to: cluster installation (including gitops kickstart), centralized VM management tool (optional). In general the responsibilities are so limited that one can question the value of having ACM (I certainly am doing that). Michele would also like to activate the centralized observability capability.", "type": "message", "user": "Raffaele Spazzoli"}
925b82a1-9193-46f4-a5f7-dd42009a8c71	ec6a32ee-dfb0-4162-97da-cc5057ded35a	1778199423.000003	Patricia de las Heras Granaglia	[Migration Update]\nNo activity during Hypercare. I'm going to share the proactive support case with TAMs\nPilot Phase 1: continues today: 1 VM. Retrospective to be run by the end of the week\nPilot Phase 2: Still working on scope. Vinay is pushing to speed up the process and start migrations ASAP, proposing skipping ITSO approvals. He is even pushing to star migrating whatever is on categories 1,2,3 in UK in the Fabric 111, without following the process defined by Accenture. Which Damon is trying to avoid. Because migrating at scale at this stage is a bit risky.\nCountry Pilot: Working on scope for UK and 3 fabrics (not available now, but dates to be provided by Vinay)	{"ts": "1778199423.000003", "text": "[Migration Update]\\nNo activity during Hypercare. I'm going to share the proactive support case with TAMs\\nPilot Phase 1: continues today: 1 VM. Retrospective to be run by the end of the week\\nPilot Phase 2: Still working on scope. Vinay is pushing to speed up the process and start migrations ASAP, proposing skipping ITSO approvals. He is even pushing to star migrating whatever is on categories 1,2,3 in UK in the Fabric 111, without following the process defined by Accenture. Which Damon is trying to avoid. Because migrating at scale at this stage is a bit risky.\\nCountry Pilot: Working on scope for UK and 3 fabrics (not available now, but dates to be provided by Vinay)", "type": "message", "user": "Patricia de las Heras Granaglia"}
cd7413e8-834e-4f13-8628-ac4f88ba64d2	e9ca8050-ffb7-4246-8dae-7d7696f97244	1778200444.000004	Sam Marland	I'm putting together a list of people that have made a significant supporting contribution to EOS. Please can you add anyone we're missing: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KIJwEZnw1vKQqUX9Hn99LnjPm7LEYckTT3Xsmli4Yhw/edit?tab=t.0	{"ts": "1778200444.000004", "text": "I'm putting together a list of people that have made a significant supporting contribution to EOS. Please can you add anyone we're missing: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KIJwEZnw1vKQqUX9Hn99LnjPm7LEYckTT3Xsmli4Yhw/edit?tab=t.0", "type": "message", "user": "Sam Marland"}
256fbe90-e45d-402a-a5bd-b56052e09b8a	f25f6990-87c7-4b7d-8b38-e96fc60e3fb0	1778203325.000005	Alexander Rydekull	@Philip Jones @James Force @rspazzol How did the initial enrollment with Vault look like now? A new cluster is created, we generate something on our end, and then send it to Vault team who adds our vault? or something like that, right? Then we have a vault connected to our cluster, and then we consume secrets from there.	{"ts": "1778203325.000005", "text": "@Philip Jones @James Force @rspazzol How did the initial enrollment with Vault look like now? A new cluster is created, we generate something on our end, and then send it to Vault team who adds our vault? or something like that, right? Then we have a vault connected to our cluster, and then we consume secrets from there.", "type": "message", "user": "Alexander Rydekull"}
c768654c-3a1f-4de3-a6ad-a1b6ee5b95cb	c1712ff9-d9de-4d49-8470-d59661a3d518	1778207166.000006	Patricia de las Heras Granaglia	[Migration Event Pilot Phase 1 #2]\nSuccesfully migrated the VM in scope for today, for the service Enterprise-Telephony-UK-Dev. Service Validation completed succesfully\nNext Migration Event -> Wednesday Apr 15th, Scope: 9 VMs	{"ts": "1778207166.000006", "text": "[Migration Event Pilot Phase 1 #2]\\nSuccesfully migrated the VM in scope for today, for the service Enterprise-Telephony-UK-Dev. Service Validation completed succesfully\\nNext Migration Event -> Wednesday Apr 15th, Scope: 9 VMs", "type": "message", "user": "Patricia de las Heras Granaglia"}
