497be85c-9262-4653-b0e6-82bdfe84f64a	6ce8e077-1d45-478e-804a-bf3f6a4fac9f	1778212290.000030	Patricia de las Heras Granaglia	[Migration Retro Call]\nSTOP. Waiting for people to show up at the Incident Bridge. Agreed to establish a reasonable time and if no one shows up, decide to roll back\nSTOP. Bringing so many people to the call to troubleshoot.\nSTART. SRE Team should have access to OCP, VCenter, etc, etc (some of them did not have)\nSTART? Shall Red Hat have a SNOW group so they can assign icidents to us? I've asked ACN to confirm with Vinay first\nRCA and propose a solution (if it ends up being  platform incompatibility). I've updated the proactive support case to see if we can get help from Support, and Shebi is collecting a list of similar issues and solutions	{"ts": "1778212290.000030", "text": "[Migration Retro Call]\\nSTOP. Waiting for people to show up at the Incident Bridge. Agreed to establish a reasonable time and if no one shows up, decide to roll back\\nSTOP. Bringing so many people to the call to troubleshoot.\\nSTART. SRE Team should have access to OCP, VCenter, etc, etc (some of them did not have)\\nSTART? Shall Red Hat have a SNOW group so they can assign icidents to us? I've asked ACN to confirm with Vinay first\\nRCA and propose a solution (if it ends up being  platform incompatibility). I've updated the proactive support case to see if we can get help from Support, and Shebi is collecting a list of similar issues and solutions", "type": "message", "user": "Patricia de las Heras Granaglia"}
5b9cca9d-9596-43b7-a1b9-b64ed9543850	5ae678ff-93a3-4b67-841e-f953179d3cd4	1778216311.000031	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Affinity and Anti-Affinity rules] As @Petros recently highlighted, we currently have a gap regarding VM management post-migration. Right now, when a VM is migrated via MTV, its definition and affinity rules live exclusively inside the OpenShift cluster’s etcd database. If a VM is deleted, those placement rules are permanently lost because we currently lack a central source of truth outside of the hypervisor to manage them declaratively.\nIn a meeting with Michele, Jakob, Phil and @Rydekull we agreed that addressing this gap and programmatically managing these rules represents a net-new requirement for the platform. Here is the breakdown of our current process, the future state, and the impact on backups.\nCurrent State (Manual):\nA user submits a ServiceNow request specifying their Anti-Affinity needs.\nA VMware Admin (OpenShift Admin in future) takes that request and manually updates the target Virtual Machines with the specific rules.\nData Protection & Label Recovery:\nAutomated Recovery: The backup solution will only be able to automatically restore VM labels (used for Anti-Affinity) in environments where the workflow explicitly backs up the VM Object, Jakob confirmed this is not the case for all clusters.\nManual Recovery: For any other setup, the recovery process falls back on the VM Admin to restore the VM (From the golden image + restore from Netbackup) and manually reapply the labels (Using ServiceNow tickets regarding the Virtual Machine to be recovered).\nFuture State (Data Architecture):\nTo solve this, the target design is to build an externalized config management system (leveraging GitOps). This will serve as the central source of truth for Anti-Affinity rules data, which will then automatically push updates and reconcile the state down to the OpenShift clusters. This is not part of our current project, and it will be discussed at a later stage.\nNext Steps:\nI am going to add the backup/restore limitations to the “Data Protection and Data Recovery” section of our documentation, and I will add the externalized config management system to the “Data Architecture” section as a future requirement.	{"ts": "1778216311.000031", "text": "[Affinity and Anti-Affinity rules] As @Petros recently highlighted, we currently have a gap regarding VM management post-migration. Right now, when a VM is migrated via MTV, its definition and affinity rules live exclusively inside the OpenShift cluster’s etcd database. If a VM is deleted, those placement rules are permanently lost because we currently lack a central source of truth outside of the hypervisor to manage them declaratively.\\nIn a meeting with Michele, Jakob, Phil and @Rydekull we agreed that addressing this gap and programmatically managing these rules represents a net-new requirement for the platform. Here is the breakdown of our current process, the future state, and the impact on backups.\\nCurrent State (Manual):\\nA user submits a ServiceNow request specifying their Anti-Affinity needs.\\nA VMware Admin (OpenShift Admin in future) takes that request and manually updates the target Virtual Machines with the specific rules.\\nData Protection & Label Recovery:\\nAutomated Recovery: The backup solution will only be able to automatically restore VM labels (used for Anti-Affinity) in environments where the workflow explicitly backs up the VM Object, Jakob confirmed this is not the case for all clusters.\\nManual Recovery: For any other setup, the recovery process falls back on the VM Admin to restore the VM (From the golden image + restore from Netbackup) and manually reapply the labels (Using ServiceNow tickets regarding the Virtual Machine to be recovered).\\nFuture State (Data Architecture):\\nTo solve this, the target design is to build an externalized config management system (leveraging GitOps). This will serve as the central source of truth for Anti-Affinity rules data, which will then automatically push updates and reconcile the state down to the OpenShift clusters. This is not part of our current project, and it will be discussed at a later stage.\\nNext Steps:\\nI am going to add the backup/restore limitations to the “Data Protection and Data Recovery” section of our documentation, and I will add the externalized config management system to the “Data Architecture” section as a future requirement.", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
