c75748f8-eb4c-41fc-aba0-4619eb7a03ba	2eeeb51b-6b20-4f23-8052-b46ceee45377	1778238878.000038	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Design Workstream - OpenShift Control Plane with Michele]: Hey team, Here is a summary of today’s meeting with Michele, @Rydekull, and @janine regarding the OpenShift Virtualization Control Plane architectures and our strategy for the bank’s infrastructure.\n1. Control Plane Topologies in Scope\nMichele outlined the architectural patterns we are considering, categorized by site constraints and stretching requirements:\n• Non-stretched, non-constrained sites (local pattern):\nStandard cluster (3-node dedicated control plane)\nCompact cluster (3-node converged control plane + workloads)\n• Stretched patterns, non-constrained sites:\n2-Node Arbiter (1 CP per main site + arbiter in a 3rd site)\nSite Affinity CPs (Stretched OSV cluster across Primary + Secondary DC, pinning CPs to 1 site; etcd on active/active storage)\nCluster stretched across 3 sites (Control plane distributed in each site - dedicated or converged)\n5-Node Control Plane (3 CPs in Primary and 2 CPs in Secondary DC)\n• Non-stretched, constrained sites (edge/small footprint):\nSingle Node OpenShift (SNO)\nHosted Control Planes / HyperShift (Per-site management cluster hosting multiple HCPs)\nDC-hosted HCP for constrained sites (workers at the edge site, control plane hosted in a hub DC)\n2-Node arbiter in a third site (exception-based)\nRemote Worker Nodes\n2. VMware “Single/Double Clusters” vs. OpenShift Reorganization\nWe discussed the bank’s current VMware deployment model, which strictly relies on “Single” and “Double” clusters. We agreed that this legacy pattern does not apply to OpenShift. Because OpenShift does not mandate a two-node minimum for a cluster (e.g., we can use SNO or 3-node compact), we have the opportunity to completely reorganize the infrastructure to maximize resource efficiency rather than mirroring the VMware footprint 1:1.\nFor context, here is the bank’s current VMware footprint that we will be reorganizing:\nAmerica: 5 Double Clusters (Total: 5)\nAsia: 54 Double, 3 Single Clusters (Total: 57)\nEurope: 24 Double, 10 Single Clusters (Total: 34)\n3. Action Items (Documentation)\n@janine will take the lead on documenting two architectural topics:\nControl Plane Protection: How OpenShift safeguards the control plane in scenarios involving memory compression or Pod eviction.\nSNO Disaster Recovery: The exact recovery process for migrating VMs from one Single Node OpenShift instance to another SNO instance in the event of a catastrophe, specifically leveraging a shared storage backplane between the two instances.\nI wanted to give created some diagrams about each scenario, I started to use the deck created by @rspazzol for it. https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1pzeLapP79fwMHs1KosbonrhNaL4ka62rTKFt4TeKaVE/edit?slide=id.g3d5d185d837_0_946#slide=id.g3d5d185d837_0_946 (edited) \nGoogle Slides	{"ts": "1778238878.000038", "text": "[Design Workstream - OpenShift Control Plane with Michele]: Hey team, Here is a summary of today’s meeting with Michele, @Rydekull, and @janine regarding the OpenShift Virtualization Control Plane architectures and our strategy for the bank’s infrastructure.\\n1. Control Plane Topologies in Scope\\nMichele outlined the architectural patterns we are considering, categorized by site constraints and stretching requirements:\\n• Non-stretched, non-constrained sites (local pattern):\\nStandard cluster (3-node dedicated control plane)\\nCompact cluster (3-node converged control plane + workloads)\\n• Stretched patterns, non-constrained sites:\\n2-Node Arbiter (1 CP per main site + arbiter in a 3rd site)\\nSite Affinity CPs (Stretched OSV cluster across Primary + Secondary DC, pinning CPs to 1 site; etcd on active/active storage)\\nCluster stretched across 3 sites (Control plane distributed in each site - dedicated or converged)\\n5-Node Control Plane (3 CPs in Primary and 2 CPs in Secondary DC)\\n• Non-stretched, constrained sites (edge/small footprint):\\nSingle Node OpenShift (SNO)\\nHosted Control Planes / HyperShift (Per-site management cluster hosting multiple HCPs)\\nDC-hosted HCP for constrained sites (workers at the edge site, control plane hosted in a hub DC)\\n2-Node arbiter in a third site (exception-based)\\nRemote Worker Nodes\\n2. VMware “Single/Double Clusters” vs. OpenShift Reorganization\\nWe discussed the bank’s current VMware deployment model, which strictly relies on “Single” and “Double” clusters. We agreed that this legacy pattern does not apply to OpenShift. Because OpenShift does not mandate a two-node minimum for a cluster (e.g., we can use SNO or 3-node compact), we have the opportunity to completely reorganize the infrastructure to maximize resource efficiency rather than mirroring the VMware footprint 1:1.\\nFor context, here is the bank’s current VMware footprint that we will be reorganizing:\\nAmerica: 5 Double Clusters (Total: 5)\\nAsia: 54 Double, 3 Single Clusters (Total: 57)\\nEurope: 24 Double, 10 Single Clusters (Total: 34)\\n3. Action Items (Documentation)\\n@janine will take the lead on documenting two architectural topics:\\nControl Plane Protection: How OpenShift safeguards the control plane in scenarios involving memory compression or Pod eviction.\\nSNO Disaster Recovery: The exact recovery process for migrating VMs from one Single Node OpenShift instance to another SNO instance in the event of a catastrophe, specifically leveraging a shared storage backplane between the two instances.\\nI wanted to give created some diagrams about each scenario, I started to use the deck created by @rspazzol for it. https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1pzeLapP79fwMHs1KosbonrhNaL4ka62rTKFt4TeKaVE/edit?slide=id.g3d5d185d837_0_946#slide=id.g3d5d185d837_0_946 (edited) \\nGoogle Slides", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
dd78fa70-2a42-49f0-bc52-d4f4d2f89e63	1d759c1e-271b-40d9-89c2-d37a2fb8390d	1778239899.000039	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Migration Workstream - update]:\nWindows Golden Image Integration: The team is taking the configurations we developed for the Windows golden image (such as restricting qemu-agent RPC calls and configuring the VirtIO drivers via the VM YAML) and baking them directly into the migration automation to prevent post-migration issues.\nCurrent Status: They are implementing these features now and will report back if they need further assistance or troubleshooting from our side.\nUS Migration Workstream & IP Updates (Today’s Sync), we had a meeting with @janine , @jfeind, @Rydekull with the team about this topic.\nRole Update: Narender officially designated me as the owner from our side for the US Migration Workstream. Was this discussed with you @pdelashe?\nIP Change Matrix: The Migration team is creating a testing matrix to handle IP address changes during the datacenter moves, covering every OS variant and scenarios with single or multiple network interfaces.\nAutomation Strategy: We agreed that Ansible is the best path forward to automate these post-migration IP updates. We offered our help to develop and refine this automation.\nAction Items & Next Steps\nTo build and validate the Ansible playbooks, we need live test environments.\nSrinath is currently taking the task to provision the test machines. I explicitly requested at least one Windows VM and one Linux VM so we can validate the automation logic across both OS variants. He will notify us as soon as they are ready for us to begin testing. I will need someone to pick this up once they deliver the VMs to test @Petros or @jfeind are you up for this one?	{"ts": "1778239899.000039", "text": "[Migration Workstream - update]:\\nWindows Golden Image Integration: The team is taking the configurations we developed for the Windows golden image (such as restricting qemu-agent RPC calls and configuring the VirtIO drivers via the VM YAML) and baking them directly into the migration automation to prevent post-migration issues.\\nCurrent Status: They are implementing these features now and will report back if they need further assistance or troubleshooting from our side.\\nUS Migration Workstream & IP Updates (Today’s Sync), we had a meeting with @janine , @jfeind, @Rydekull with the team about this topic.\\nRole Update: Narender officially designated me as the owner from our side for the US Migration Workstream. Was this discussed with you @pdelashe?\\nIP Change Matrix: The Migration team is creating a testing matrix to handle IP address changes during the datacenter moves, covering every OS variant and scenarios with single or multiple network interfaces.\\nAutomation Strategy: We agreed that Ansible is the best path forward to automate these post-migration IP updates. We offered our help to develop and refine this automation.\\nAction Items & Next Steps\\nTo build and validate the Ansible playbooks, we need live test environments.\\nSrinath is currently taking the task to provision the test machines. I explicitly requested at least one Windows VM and one Linux VM so we can validate the automation logic across both OS variants. He will notify us as soon as they are ready for us to begin testing. I will need someone to pick this up once they deliver the VMs to test @Petros or @jfeind are you up for this one?", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
8bda2fa3-90dd-408f-a6bb-a10b265bfeb7	5e244209-4da3-4285-9bf6-da06e73a3ac0	1778197300.000040	Jens Feind	Regarding GitOps: Can we please establish a system where a PR is to be reviewed by one Red Hatter and one HSBC Team member? Also I saw that on my recent PR the Reviewer just added 3 more Commits putting in some extra values which then merged outside the feedback loop.\nThat is in my humble opinion not a recommended practice of a Review process. :expressionless:	{"ts": "1778197300.000040", "text": "Regarding GitOps: Can we please establish a system where a PR is to be reviewed by one Red Hatter and one HSBC Team member? Also I saw that on my recent PR the Reviewer just added 3 more Commits putting in some extra values which then merged outside the feedback loop.\\nThat is in my humble opinion not a recommended practice of a Review process. :expressionless:", "type": "message", "user": "Jens Feind"}
29601e6b-60b0-4813-9a71-5613eb2032df	3a570333-32a9-439e-8986-d40353e94468	1778197481.000041	Patricia de las Heras Granaglia	[Migration update]\nPilot Phase #1\nPlanned retro for Friday -> I will share yesterday’s feedback on that meeting\n3rd migration event starts today\nPilot Phase #2\nConcerns of finding workloads: legacy, no one knows the applications\nAction for Red Hat: figure out % of legacy over installed base we have found in prior engagements\nAgree on Scope: Current volumes agreed 230VMs across 93 services\nNext Steps: Start looking at the Comms to the ITSO. MIoX does not support it yet so comms will be done manually considering sequencing communications properly to not overload ITSOs mailboxes\nDiscussion on what to prioritize in MIoX: Comms Integrations vs more patterns. The first will give us more volume than the second\nNext Steps: Baseline the plan. I’d like to be on tomorrow’s meeting\nCountry Pilot UK\nCreating the first cutoff of the plan. Will be shared by Monday. Start Date 2 weeks after completing Phase 2\nScope calculated based on : Fabric -> Services -> VM.\nVimal to provide a view of Fabric readiness for UK	{"ts": "1778197481.000041", "text": "[Migration update]\\nPilot Phase #1\\nPlanned retro for Friday -> I will share yesterday’s feedback on that meeting\\n3rd migration event starts today\\nPilot Phase #2\\nConcerns of finding workloads: legacy, no one knows the applications\\nAction for Red Hat: figure out % of legacy over installed base we have found in prior engagements\\nAgree on Scope: Current volumes agreed 230VMs across 93 services\\nNext Steps: Start looking at the Comms to the ITSO. MIoX does not support it yet so comms will be done manually considering sequencing communications properly to not overload ITSOs mailboxes\\nDiscussion on what to prioritize in MIoX: Comms Integrations vs more patterns. The first will give us more volume than the second\\nNext Steps: Baseline the plan. I’d like to be on tomorrow’s meeting\\nCountry Pilot UK\\nCreating the first cutoff of the plan. Will be shared by Monday. Start Date 2 weeks after completing Phase 2\\nScope calculated based on : Fabric -> Services -> VM.\\nVimal to provide a view of Fabric readiness for UK", "type": "message", "user": "Patricia de las Heras Granaglia"}
00c28343-4216-4340-8552-0e5e95e810ae	f84b9248-17f6-4cc7-b0be-1e8d18811612	1778197662.000042	Patricia de las Heras Granaglia	@Shebi the US Migration Workstream is an "official" workstream? With different people and diffrerent meetings? Because if YES, we need to add it to our workstream planner, at least to keep track of it and include it in reporting	{"ts": "1778197662.000042", "text": "@Shebi the US Migration Workstream is an \\"official\\" workstream? With different people and diffrerent meetings? Because if YES, we need to add it to our workstream planner, at least to keep track of it and include it in reporting", "type": "message", "user": "Patricia de las Heras Granaglia"}
