e06ab921-6e7f-438f-8cf0-83399e95c0f7	a7af5e4c-965c-47cb-9d3b-1fee5f3ae6f6	1778198363.000083	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Naming Conventions & DataSource Management] We had a meeting with @janine, JD, Nitesh, Ryan, and Samuel.\nWe started with a discussion on VM image naming conventions, which quickly transformed into an architectural conversation about how we manage DataSources, image versioning, and exceptions for specific OS requirements (especially for the Windows team).\nCurrently, clusters pull container images from Nexus, tag them as “latest,” and make them available as a DataSource. While this works for HIC environments, it does not meet the requirements for Non-HIC environments. The Windows team requires vendor-driven compatibility and the ability to use specific older image versions (N-1, N-2, etc.). Relying purely on the “latest” tag breaks the audit trail, making it difficult to track exactly which patch or build a VM was spun up from. More info in the thread --> :threeeyedfish:	{"ts": "1778198363.000083", "text": "[Naming Conventions & DataSource Management] We had a meeting with @janine, JD, Nitesh, Ryan, and Samuel.\\nWe started with a discussion on VM image naming conventions, which quickly transformed into an architectural conversation about how we manage DataSources, image versioning, and exceptions for specific OS requirements (especially for the Windows team).\\nCurrently, clusters pull container images from Nexus, tag them as “latest,” and make them available as a DataSource. While this works for HIC environments, it does not meet the requirements for Non-HIC environments. The Windows team requires vendor-driven compatibility and the ability to use specific older image versions (N-1, N-2, etc.). Relying purely on the “latest” tag breaks the audit trail, making it difficult to track exactly which patch or build a VM was spun up from. More info in the thread --> :threeeyedfish:", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
