85727f2d-c9a6-4a70-9940-ab4fc5138937	e8628613-e516-4708-957d-57523f658a89	1778221548.000108	Philip Jones	@ricardo gregorio just an FYI that the Dell CSM driver has been successfully updated on 104 now. Hopefully everything needed is there and working.	{"ts": "1778221548.000108", "text": "@ricardo gregorio just an FYI that the Dell CSM driver has been successfully updated on 104 now. Hopefully everything needed is there and working.", "type": "message", "user": "Philip Jones"}
7ebbe39c-66b8-42e3-bd97-44621c4fecd7	80517352-3617-4fef-ba50-2cd0b4a92ea7	1778227129.000109	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Migration Workstream - US]:\nMoving the estate to the new datacenter requires a massive re-configuration of IP addresses across the board. Currently, the end-to-end automation required to orchestrate this does not exist.\nI have started a Proof of Concept (PoC) to automate the IP address changes directly at the VM OS level. For now, this PoC operates in isolation, without factoring in the broader external network dependencies (DNS, Load Balancers, etc.).\nThe Target End-to-End Workflow:\nUltimately, we need to build and connect automation that handles the full lifecycle:\nVM Migration -> Firewall Updates -> DNS Changes -> Load Balancer Changes\nAppliances & Load Balancers:\nScott J Wright is driving the appliances migrations. Currently, there is some self-service automation for F5 load balancers (for creating and deleting LBs) that a developer is working on. However, how this specifically fits into the  migration  is still unclear.\nAction Items & Next Steps:\nI proposed that we work directly with Scott to fully understand the capabilities of the existing F5 automation. (If anyone wants to join tomorrow let me know, 3:30 PM UK)\nMichael will be collaborating with @rspazzol and me to map out the broader Network automation so we can start putting all these isolated pieces together for the US migration.	{"ts": "1778227129.000109", "text": "[Migration Workstream - US]:\\nMoving the estate to the new datacenter requires a massive re-configuration of IP addresses across the board. Currently, the end-to-end automation required to orchestrate this does not exist.\\nI have started a Proof of Concept (PoC) to automate the IP address changes directly at the VM OS level. For now, this PoC operates in isolation, without factoring in the broader external network dependencies (DNS, Load Balancers, etc.).\\nThe Target End-to-End Workflow:\\nUltimately, we need to build and connect automation that handles the full lifecycle:\\nVM Migration -> Firewall Updates -> DNS Changes -> Load Balancer Changes\\nAppliances & Load Balancers:\\nScott J Wright is driving the appliances migrations. Currently, there is some self-service automation for F5 load balancers (for creating and deleting LBs) that a developer is working on. However, how this specifically fits into the  migration  is still unclear.\\nAction Items & Next Steps:\\nI proposed that we work directly with Scott to fully understand the capabilities of the existing F5 automation. (If anyone wants to join tomorrow let me know, 3:30 PM UK)\\nMichael will be collaborating with @rspazzol and me to map out the broader Network automation so we can start putting all these isolated pieces together for the US migration.", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
56333341-454d-443f-96ab-600f416a5264	98c3e454-1563-49d6-862d-a7c3713c0da4	1778228390.000110	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Automation WorkGroup Discussion (Gareth pull me on this call)] - This is in line with the Data architecture workstream I am currently working into:\nIT Service Portal (ITSP) to OpenShift Integration\nCurrently, the IT Service Portal offering only supports VMware for VM provisioning and VM-level automation (such as resizing CPU and memory). Nilesh is working on extending this capability to OpenShift, and these features have been added to the platform backlog.\nData Governance and Advanced VM Settings\nI raised concerns regarding the end-to-end data governance from the ITSP portal to actual VM provisioning. While basic compute resizing is in the backlog for OpenShift, not all VMware settings will translate directly. I emphasized the need to automate these rules, shared the relevant documentation links fro Confluence (Data Architecture Document), and offered to help consolidate and test the process.\nAction Items & Next Steps:\nGareth & Nilesh: They will have a meeting (not planed yet) to plan the next steps regarding the backlog and automation mapping for the IT Service Portal.	{"ts": "1778228390.000110", "text": "[Automation WorkGroup Discussion (Gareth pull me on this call)] - This is in line with the Data architecture workstream I am currently working into:\\nIT Service Portal (ITSP) to OpenShift Integration\\nCurrently, the IT Service Portal offering only supports VMware for VM provisioning and VM-level automation (such as resizing CPU and memory). Nilesh is working on extending this capability to OpenShift, and these features have been added to the platform backlog.\\nData Governance and Advanced VM Settings\\nI raised concerns regarding the end-to-end data governance from the ITSP portal to actual VM provisioning. While basic compute resizing is in the backlog for OpenShift, not all VMware settings will translate directly. I emphasized the need to automate these rules, shared the relevant documentation links fro Confluence (Data Architecture Document), and offered to help consolidate and test the process.\\nAction Items & Next Steps:\\nGareth & Nilesh: They will have a meeting (not planed yet) to plan the next steps regarding the backlog and automation mapping for the IT Service Portal.", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
a145abcd-2369-4876-a684-2c4720ee643e	ea8f3a4a-6867-49be-aa2e-dbe51edef118	1778230071.000111	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Sheffield’s Training Feedback]: I want to share some valuable feedback I just received from one of our  students regarding the current OpenShift Virtualization training plan. This aligns perfectly with what we have been hearing from others who have gone through the recent sessions @Philip Jones@James Force@Nikos Help me out if I am missing or misinforming something, you were there present on the trainings.\nWhile the standard Red Hat hands-on labs (DO156/DO316) are excellent for baseline knowledge, there is a significant disconnect between the “textbook” OpenShift platform and the bank’s actual operational reality. The trainees are having a hard time mapping the generic labs to our bespoke environment.\nThe training assumes standard platform usage (e.g., using the OpenShift Web Console and CLI). In reality, our teams will not be logging into the console. We are strictly driving operations via GitOps, not using OVN Networking for VMs, and Commvault/other tools for backups.\nArchitectural Vulnerabilities Exposed: Seeing the standard platform behavior actually reinforced a gap we discussed recently: currently, VM configurations (scheduling, labeling, annotations, affinity rules) only live in the OpenShift etcd database. The trainees rightfully pointed out that we need to see how this infrastructure deployment looks in GitOps/YAML format to truly manage it.\nWe need a shortcut to bridge the gap between what the vanilla platform offers and how we actually build and operate it here.\n@kibrahim - Given this feedback, what can we do to adapt the upcoming enablement phases?\nWould it be possible to inject a custom bridging session into the curriculum? The Architects and Consultants could host a walk-through to connect the Red Hat training concepts directly to the bank’s GitOps architecture so the trainees get a realistic view of their day-to-day operations.	{"ts": "1778230071.000111", "text": "[Sheffield’s Training Feedback]: I want to share some valuable feedback I just received from one of our  students regarding the current OpenShift Virtualization training plan. This aligns perfectly with what we have been hearing from others who have gone through the recent sessions @Philip Jones@James Force@Nikos Help me out if I am missing or misinforming something, you were there present on the trainings.\\nWhile the standard Red Hat hands-on labs (DO156/DO316) are excellent for baseline knowledge, there is a significant disconnect between the “textbook” OpenShift platform and the bank’s actual operational reality. The trainees are having a hard time mapping the generic labs to our bespoke environment.\\nThe training assumes standard platform usage (e.g., using the OpenShift Web Console and CLI). In reality, our teams will not be logging into the console. We are strictly driving operations via GitOps, not using OVN Networking for VMs, and Commvault/other tools for backups.\\nArchitectural Vulnerabilities Exposed: Seeing the standard platform behavior actually reinforced a gap we discussed recently: currently, VM configurations (scheduling, labeling, annotations, affinity rules) only live in the OpenShift etcd database. The trainees rightfully pointed out that we need to see how this infrastructure deployment looks in GitOps/YAML format to truly manage it.\\nWe need a shortcut to bridge the gap between what the vanilla platform offers and how we actually build and operate it here.\\n@kibrahim - Given this feedback, what can we do to adapt the upcoming enablement phases?\\nWould it be possible to inject a custom bridging session into the curriculum? The Architects and Consultants could host a walk-through to connect the Red Hat training concepts directly to the bank’s GitOps architecture so the trainees get a realistic view of their day-to-day operations.", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
671ccf9f-e273-4bd9-a4c6-2bfbe81a35e8	e28233d0-18fd-4ccf-aeac-6c62ce9ccff7	1778218263.000123	Raffaele Spazzoli	[constrained countries OCP topology] based on dennis feedback that he does not want 1- or 2- node clusters, the recommendation is to purchase all new HW to be able to deploy 3 node clusters everywhere. should be around 100x3 servers, something like that. Bottom line for us, we might not need to support remote workers. Rushi was happy about it.	{"ts": "1778218263.000123", "text": "[constrained countries OCP topology] based on dennis feedback that he does not want 1- or 2- node clusters, the recommendation is to purchase all new HW to be able to deploy 3 node clusters everywhere. should be around 100x3 servers, something like that. Bottom line for us, we might not need to support remote workers. Rushi was happy about it.", "type": "message", "user": "Raffaele Spazzoli"}
f89cbc4d-7c2b-4d6d-b1f9-a197689c5850	b7d1504f-427b-48fe-ad89-4e19b64b1d1e	1778231452.000112	Philip Jones	[PJ build] -\nUpdated Dell CSM to latest version on 104. Now successful following use of additionalImages to pull all the missing images, and then create a custom configmap to reference them all.\nFix ACM search on 101 and created a new PR to create a search pvc on Pure storage.\nWork on adding details to issues\nReviewing required changes for Pure Storage to set max limit for PVC's per node.\nCalled into an Entra troubleshooting call for 301. Ongoing....	{"ts": "1778231452.000112", "text": "[PJ build] -\\nUpdated Dell CSM to latest version on 104. Now successful following use of additionalImages to pull all the missing images, and then create a custom configmap to reference them all.\\nFix ACM search on 101 and created a new PR to create a search pvc on Pure storage.\\nWork on adding details to issues\\nReviewing required changes for Pure Storage to set max limit for PVC's per node.\\nCalled into an Entra troubleshooting call for 301. Ongoing....", "type": "message", "user": "Philip Jones"}
9c1f2a4a-244f-4e3f-a87f-4b7d04827cdd	b5c58945-edc3-4e79-9097-a758b3ce93a4	1778198273.000113	Patricia de las Heras Granaglia	[Migration Update]\nPilot Phase 2 includes only DEV, UAT and OAT (Operational Acceptance Test) VMS and will be run during mornings (UK Time) so no need to follow-the-sun model yet\nDetailed plan covering Pilot Phase #2 and Country Pilot (UK) will be shared on the Red Hat Channel on Teams - plan covers almost 3000 VMs migrated by Mid June. This plan assumes that 2 more clusters will be ready by mid and end of may for fabrics 113 and 122. By June 15th everything should be ready to start migrating Contingency and Production VMs.\nAs soon as we have the “committed” dates for HK clusters for the selected fabrics, the planning for HK should start\nHypercare: There is a meeting today between Chris, Vinay & ACN to discuss Hypercare. We have intentionally been left out of this meeting\nReminders:\n@Shebi  to send the list of “common issues found during migrations” that he extracted from support cases to the VM Migration Workstream\n@pdelashe to share with Damon and Viky the analysis of failure ratios in other projects (to be reviewed by Brenna first)	{"ts": "1778198273.000113", "text": "[Migration Update]\\nPilot Phase 2 includes only DEV, UAT and OAT (Operational Acceptance Test) VMS and will be run during mornings (UK Time) so no need to follow-the-sun model yet\\nDetailed plan covering Pilot Phase #2 and Country Pilot (UK) will be shared on the Red Hat Channel on Teams - plan covers almost 3000 VMs migrated by Mid June. This plan assumes that 2 more clusters will be ready by mid and end of may for fabrics 113 and 122. By June 15th everything should be ready to start migrating Contingency and Production VMs.\\nAs soon as we have the “committed” dates for HK clusters for the selected fabrics, the planning for HK should start\\nHypercare: There is a meeting today between Chris, Vinay & ACN to discuss Hypercare. We have intentionally been left out of this meeting\\nReminders:\\n@Shebi  to send the list of “common issues found during migrations” that he extracted from support cases to the VM Migration Workstream\\n@pdelashe to share with Damon and Viky the analysis of failure ratios in other projects (to be reviewed by Brenna first)", "type": "message", "user": "Patricia de las Heras Granaglia"}
4c7b1bd0-d913-41b0-9685-2b6055bbdb57	d02b1a8e-85e2-420d-b910-d94bfd5f20c0	1778199834.000114	Philip Jones	Morning all, just to confirm that EntraID issue on 301 is now confirmed as a proxy config issue. It isn't permitting traffic to graph.microsoft.com which is a requirement. Why they didn't just copy what they had enabled on the old proxies I have no idea. Regardless it will now be a change request that has to go through a CAB apparently so not a quick fix :disappointed:	{"ts": "1778199834.000114", "text": "Morning all, just to confirm that EntraID issue on 301 is now confirmed as a proxy config issue. It isn't permitting traffic to graph.microsoft.com which is a requirement. Why they didn't just copy what they had enabled on the old proxies I have no idea. Regardless it will now be a change request that has to go through a CAB apparently so not a quick fix :disappointed:", "type": "message", "user": "Philip Jones"}
9b686317-f3db-4952-9b2e-ecfe459f541b	3b26393f-4a1b-4a96-accb-2174479ebcd0	1778201515.000115	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Build Standup Call]: @Philip Jones keep me honest here, so we give more context to @janine before joining the call. For building new clusters with ACM the secret is getting refreshed unexpectedly, causing the InfraEnv variables and the ISO image to fluctuate. Because the ISO keeps changing, the ISO attachment process frequently fails, throwing “ISO is not ready” errors. the team is relying on a manual process to create and apply these secrets to bypass the failures. To fix this, we need to finalize our automated secret management strategy. We need an automated way to pull these secrets, integrate them with Vault for proper password rotation, and reliably replicate them down to the managed clusters without breaking the deployment flow.	{"ts": "1778201515.000115", "text": "[Build Standup Call]: @Philip Jones keep me honest here, so we give more context to @janine before joining the call. For building new clusters with ACM the secret is getting refreshed unexpectedly, causing the InfraEnv variables and the ISO image to fluctuate. Because the ISO keeps changing, the ISO attachment process frequently fails, throwing “ISO is not ready” errors. the team is relying on a manual process to create and apply these secrets to bypass the failures. To fix this, we need to finalize our automated secret management strategy. We need an automated way to pull these secrets, integrate them with Vault for proper password rotation, and reliably replicate them down to the managed clusters without breaking the deployment flow.", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
