05601859-78af-47c6-bec6-d15434d72caa	5c76ca12-05ca-4e4a-abcd-58dbb269137d	1778226665.000125	Philip Jones	Build Update\nDELL CSM Updated on 104\nNow includes OTEL metrics, credit to @ricardo gregorio\nMeeting to be scheduled by Dell for next week to discuss operator improvements.\nLatest EntraID issue identified and fix is pending a proxy change for cluster 301.\nPortworx operator upgrade prepared. We are just waiting on some required images to mirror now.	{"ts": "1778226665.000125", "text": "Build Update\\nDELL CSM Updated on 104\\nNow includes OTEL metrics, credit to @ricardo gregorio\\nMeeting to be scheduled by Dell for next week to discuss operator improvements.\\nLatest EntraID issue identified and fix is pending a proxy change for cluster 301.\\nPortworx operator upgrade prepared. We are just waiting on some required images to mirror now.", "type": "message", "user": "Philip Jones"}
b6bd89c5-7d0b-4ce1-b1f3-ff1bdccffa18	3eccf105-e18a-4302-8a52-f87638cb9b36	1778228406.000126	Ricardo Gregorio	[observability]\nadded couple of namespaces and a message keyword to the existing filter setup in the logforwarder resource;\nteam work with @Philip Jones to get the dell powermax metrics available;\nadded couple of powermax metrics to the otel configuration on 104. observability team with validate data flowing into splunk tomorrow.\nenabled, manually on 104, servicemonitor to make Trident-metrics available. At quick glance could not work out array capacity;\nthanks @Philip Jones for pulling metrics list from 104 manually;\nraised PR to enable custom alerts to monitor OTEL Collector health, message queue usage and failure of messages not queuing locally or not sending to the OTEL Collector Gateway.	{"ts": "1778228406.000126", "text": "[observability]\\nadded couple of namespaces and a message keyword to the existing filter setup in the logforwarder resource;\\nteam work with @Philip Jones to get the dell powermax metrics available;\\nadded couple of powermax metrics to the otel configuration on 104. observability team with validate data flowing into splunk tomorrow.\\nenabled, manually on 104, servicemonitor to make Trident-metrics available. At quick glance could not work out array capacity;\\nthanks @Philip Jones for pulling metrics list from 104 manually;\\nraised PR to enable custom alerts to monitor OTEL Collector health, message queue usage and failure of messages not queuing locally or not sending to the OTEL Collector Gateway.", "type": "message", "user": "Ricardo Gregorio"}
2f6982da-d393-4a62-b9d6-56d6bfdbd430	ae6e893d-28d0-4cea-98bd-fd10548306ab	1778228767.000127	Jens Feind	@Rydekull @Shebi\nSo regarding Sudhirs request on the failed migration of Windows Failover Cluster we made some progress over the day:\nProblem 1 solved: The migration itself is now successfull with all PVCs being available to both VMs. :check:\nMulti rooted issues, the migration plan execution order and configuration. Also the disks have been there once and then recreated as copy-[pvc-name..] which led to the VMs had different set of disks mounted.\nProblem 2 solved: The VMs do boot up now successfully after the Migration. :check:\nIssue was wrong NAD mappings and one NAD missing in the destination namespace.\nNew problem: The Windows Failover Cluster itself fails, I have some experience with Windows Server from 10+ years ago, nevertheless I wondered where do we draw the line in supporting applications after the migration.\nAnyway, when trying to bring the disks online it just says device i/o error, the Windows OS does see the disks but can not communicate... totally sounds like the good old SCSI3-PR issue has another place in the spotlight... ?	{"ts": "1778228767.000127", "text": "@Rydekull @Shebi\\nSo regarding Sudhirs request on the failed migration of Windows Failover Cluster we made some progress over the day:\\nProblem 1 solved: The migration itself is now successfull with all PVCs being available to both VMs. :check:\\nMulti rooted issues, the migration plan execution order and configuration. Also the disks have been there once and then recreated as copy-[pvc-name..] which led to the VMs had different set of disks mounted.\\nProblem 2 solved: The VMs do boot up now successfully after the Migration. :check:\\nIssue was wrong NAD mappings and one NAD missing in the destination namespace.\\nNew problem: The Windows Failover Cluster itself fails, I have some experience with Windows Server from 10+ years ago, nevertheless I wondered where do we draw the line in supporting applications after the migration.\\nAnyway, when trying to bring the disks online it just says device i/o error, the Windows OS does see the disks but can not communicate... totally sounds like the good old SCSI3-PR issue has another place in the spotlight... ?", "type": "message", "user": "Jens Feind"}
85a9d21b-f64e-4474-a61b-a1e2f4cc8e6a	66bc63f5-d7b4-4c2d-ba28-25ac0cb3a3f6	1778229368.000128	Arpit Tolani	I was pulled into a call regarding Microsoft Entra ID authentication failing on one cluster. On that cluster, the customer had a production proxy configured, and authentication was not working.\nThe same Entra ID setup was configured on another cluster using a different proxy, where authentication was working as expected.\nDuring debugging, I found that the non-working cluster was unable to reach Microsoft Graph (graph.microsoft.com), which was being blocked by the proxy.\nAs a temporary workaround, I suggested allowing access to that endpoint. I also advised the customer to open a support case with Microsoft or obtain an official list of required URLs. Since dependencies may change (for example, due to outages or load balancing across different endpoints), relying on a single allowed domain may not be sufficient—it's better to follow Microsoft’s documented and supported endpoint list.	{"ts": "1778229368.000128", "text": "I was pulled into a call regarding Microsoft Entra ID authentication failing on one cluster. On that cluster, the customer had a production proxy configured, and authentication was not working.\\nThe same Entra ID setup was configured on another cluster using a different proxy, where authentication was working as expected.\\nDuring debugging, I found that the non-working cluster was unable to reach Microsoft Graph (graph.microsoft.com), which was being blocked by the proxy.\\nAs a temporary workaround, I suggested allowing access to that endpoint. I also advised the customer to open a support case with Microsoft or obtain an official list of required URLs. Since dependencies may change (for example, due to outages or load balancing across different endpoints), relying on a single allowed domain may not be sufficient—it's better to follow Microsoft’s documented and supported endpoint list.", "type": "message", "user": "Arpit Tolani"}
8d8fee70-4a29-40d9-9a5f-ca99c97a0501	967bab08-60b0-4e3f-821b-35e6b8a0fa36	1778231529.000129	Carina Almeida	And why are you working at this time @atolani? 'go home' and speak to you tomorrow :wink:	{"ts": "1778231529.000129", "text": "And why are you working at this time @atolani? 'go home' and speak to you tomorrow :wink:", "type": "message", "user": "Carina Almeida"}
69481a36-f327-44ad-8692-9b99baa8a7ca	69ad1d3d-fe10-41a4-9086-2c2b1c8ad116	1778232490.000130	Raffaele Spazzoli	[hyper-care] we need a slide on how the hyper-care works at a high level (for us/mx deck). We also need a low-level description of the process in agreement with the customer. @Anne @Carina Almeida @pdelashe I expect this to come from you but do let me know if I am wrong. We need clarification on the following:\nhow does the customer engage with us when a migration fails\nwho is available and response time. Is the OS a matter in this decision.\ntroubleshooting environment (we cannot/should not access production) and level of collaboration with the migration team and service owners.. (RACI)	{"ts": "1778232490.000130", "text": "[hyper-care] we need a slide on how the hyper-care works at a high level (for us/mx deck). We also need a low-level description of the process in agreement with the customer. @Anne @Carina Almeida @pdelashe I expect this to come from you but do let me know if I am wrong. We need clarification on the following:\\nhow does the customer engage with us when a migration fails\\nwho is available and response time. Is the OS a matter in this decision.\\ntroubleshooting environment (we cannot/should not access production) and level of collaboration with the migration team and service owners.. (RACI)", "type": "message", "user": "Raffaele Spazzoli"}
6a6d3b9e-3032-4eec-a7cf-5120a4955ea6	c446a609-0bc4-476e-ad3b-dff23ac60ce8	1778241371.000131	Raffaele Spazzoli	[netapp and storage offload] just a heads up, customer tried to do storage offload with netapp. this of course does not work. netapp has its own tool called shift toolkit. We have communicated the situation,	{"ts": "1778241371.000131", "text": "[netapp and storage offload] just a heads up, customer tried to do storage offload with netapp. this of course does not work. netapp has its own tool called shift toolkit. We have communicated the situation,", "type": "message", "user": "Raffaele Spazzoli"}
f763c5f3-8531-406e-a62e-a8971af7cf54	e41941cf-5490-4cf0-a0a5-b9bde2d6ed1d	1778247372.000132	Raffaele Spazzoli	[VM triage process] we need to propose a triage process to the back as this is clearly not moving. @Shebi @janine, here is an idea:\nwhen a VM migration fails, the VM is rolled back and the migrated VM is put in triage.\nusing MTV, VM is moved from a production cluster/namespace to triage cluster/namespace\nuse virtctl guestfs to manipulate the VM OS and\n    a. change the VM IP to something compatible with the triage environment\n    b. enable a local account with root capability\nconfirm that the VM can start correct and local account works\n5 delete original migrated VM\ntroubleshoot the VM\n7 once the resolution is confirmed, delete the VM\nplease mentally verify these steps for both Linux and Windows OSes.\n@Rydekull I know you are mostly focused on security, but we could use your input too here. (edited)	{"ts": "1778247372.000132", "text": "[VM triage process] we need to propose a triage process to the back as this is clearly not moving. @Shebi @janine, here is an idea:\\nwhen a VM migration fails, the VM is rolled back and the migrated VM is put in triage.\\nusing MTV, VM is moved from a production cluster/namespace to triage cluster/namespace\\nuse virtctl guestfs to manipulate the VM OS and\\n    a. change the VM IP to something compatible with the triage environment\\n    b. enable a local account with root capability\\nconfirm that the VM can start correct and local account works\\n5 delete original migrated VM\\ntroubleshoot the VM\\n7 once the resolution is confirmed, delete the VM\\nplease mentally verify these steps for both Linux and Windows OSes.\\n@Rydekull I know you are mostly focused on security, but we could use your input too here. (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "Raffaele Spazzoli"}
cb6cf7da-77d3-4aca-a4ce-d0a32239ead4	51efbb33-6c08-4b28-8d6d-ad1c652d1611	1778250553.000133	James Force	[build - network build issue] something to be aware of... the network folks have made an error in the build out of  everything outside the immediate lab. The tldr is LACP hasn't been configured on the switch side, thus right now we're essentially only leveraging single ports. Michael and Rushi are aware and will sort it out so hopefully all in hand, but just sharing for visibility if the topic comes up.	{"ts": "1778250553.000133", "text": "[build - network build issue] something to be aware of... the network folks have made an error in the build out of  everything outside the immediate lab. The tldr is LACP hasn't been configured on the switch side, thus right now we're essentially only leveraging single ports. Michael and Rushi are aware and will sort it out so hopefully all in hand, but just sharing for visibility if the topic comes up.", "type": "message", "user": "James Force"}
