2aac26ba-a92b-4f81-8fae-41ae9b2f762a	481cbe07-3b95-4628-b4b4-e7b131ad3ffa	1778224012.000172	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Bulk Migration Testing with AAP]: Latency & AAP Platform: Magnus confirmed that a latency of around 400ms is acceptable, meaning no architectural changes to the AAP platform are required right now. We have also increased the UK database CPU to 18 and applied Red Hat’s fine-tuning recommendations.\nMigration SLAs & Strategy: The acceptable duration for VM migrations is set between 45 minutes and 1 hour 15 minutes. Our target split is 80% via storage offloading and 20% via cold migration.\nResource Consumption Testing: Because the Hong Kong lab is still under construction, Nitesh and Abhishek will conduct the next round of testing in the UK environment. We need to measure exact CPU, memory, and disk consumption during both Linux and Windows migrations to accurately calculate capacity needs.\nFuture Re-IP Scenarios: @janine told that future migrations requiring IP changes will involve additional pre- and post-migration automation tasks, taking longer. We will measure both standard and re-IP migration patterns and add safety buffers for unexpected scenarios.\nRecent Test Results: Abhishek confirmed that the recent 52 migrations had some failures, but they were not Ansible-related. The team is fixing these issues and will avoid problematic VM scenarios in the upcoming tests.	{"ts": "1778224012.000172", "text": "[Bulk Migration Testing with AAP]: Latency & AAP Platform: Magnus confirmed that a latency of around 400ms is acceptable, meaning no architectural changes to the AAP platform are required right now. We have also increased the UK database CPU to 18 and applied Red Hat’s fine-tuning recommendations.\\nMigration SLAs & Strategy: The acceptable duration for VM migrations is set between 45 minutes and 1 hour 15 minutes. Our target split is 80% via storage offloading and 20% via cold migration.\\nResource Consumption Testing: Because the Hong Kong lab is still under construction, Nitesh and Abhishek will conduct the next round of testing in the UK environment. We need to measure exact CPU, memory, and disk consumption during both Linux and Windows migrations to accurately calculate capacity needs.\\nFuture Re-IP Scenarios: @janine told that future migrations requiring IP changes will involve additional pre- and post-migration automation tasks, taking longer. We will measure both standard and re-IP migration patterns and add safety buffers for unexpected scenarios.\\nRecent Test Results: Abhishek confirmed that the recent 52 migrations had some failures, but they were not Ansible-related. The team is fixing these issues and will avoid problematic VM scenarios in the upcoming tests.", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
4f5e7abc-309e-4b4f-accd-fafe943f82fc	ad850b5b-cc68-4faf-a688-b05b49768db8	1778226293.000173	Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga	[Labels - Data Architecture - Affinity and Anti-Affinity Discussions]:\nPart 1: The Aspirational Design (Gareth & Michele)\nWe successfully validated a strategy to model our physical failure domains in OpenShift to enable High Availability via Affinity/Anti-Affinity rules. This relies on tagging OpenShift nodes and VMs with three critical dimensions: Country, Data Center, and Fabric.\nWe mapped out exactly how to inject these labels across three journeys using Unicorn as the primary System of Record:\nCluster Build (GitOps): Querying Unicorn via bare-metal hostnames to label OpenShift nodes.\nVM Migration (MIOX): Pulling metadata to inject labels into the VM manifests during migration.\nNew VM Provisioning (Ansible/ITSP): Having Ansible query Unicorn to label net-new VMs at creation.\nPart 2: The Reality Check (Rushi)\nWhen I reviewed this with Rushi, he rightly pointed out that while this looks great on paper, it is highly aspirational for our day-to-day operations.\nThe Bottleneck: Strict Affinity/Anti-Affinity requires heavy coordination with Application Owners to define these rules properly.\nThe Operational Reality: In the event of an outage, VM Admins are solely focused on restoring services immediately. They will not have the time to debug why a VM isn’t scheduling due to Anti-Affinity constraints.\nPart 3: The Alternative Proposal (Service Classes)\nTo solve this, Rushi proposed a much more pragmatic, DR-focused approach: Service Classes (Priority Levels).\nInstead of forcing App Owners to define complex affinity rules, we simply ask them to define the priority of their service.\nIn the event of a massive failure (e.g., node, rack, or hall), the platform aggressively evacuates and live-migrates the High Priority VMs to healthy nodes.\nLow Priority VMs are simply stopped/powered down to free up capacity for the critical workloads.	{"ts": "1778226293.000173", "text": "[Labels - Data Architecture - Affinity and Anti-Affinity Discussions]:\\nPart 1: The Aspirational Design (Gareth & Michele)\\nWe successfully validated a strategy to model our physical failure domains in OpenShift to enable High Availability via Affinity/Anti-Affinity rules. This relies on tagging OpenShift nodes and VMs with three critical dimensions: Country, Data Center, and Fabric.\\nWe mapped out exactly how to inject these labels across three journeys using Unicorn as the primary System of Record:\\nCluster Build (GitOps): Querying Unicorn via bare-metal hostnames to label OpenShift nodes.\\nVM Migration (MIOX): Pulling metadata to inject labels into the VM manifests during migration.\\nNew VM Provisioning (Ansible/ITSP): Having Ansible query Unicorn to label net-new VMs at creation.\\nPart 2: The Reality Check (Rushi)\\nWhen I reviewed this with Rushi, he rightly pointed out that while this looks great on paper, it is highly aspirational for our day-to-day operations.\\nThe Bottleneck: Strict Affinity/Anti-Affinity requires heavy coordination with Application Owners to define these rules properly.\\nThe Operational Reality: In the event of an outage, VM Admins are solely focused on restoring services immediately. They will not have the time to debug why a VM isn’t scheduling due to Anti-Affinity constraints.\\nPart 3: The Alternative Proposal (Service Classes)\\nTo solve this, Rushi proposed a much more pragmatic, DR-focused approach: Service Classes (Priority Levels).\\nInstead of forcing App Owners to define complex affinity rules, we simply ask them to define the priority of their service.\\nIn the event of a massive failure (e.g., node, rack, or hall), the platform aggressively evacuates and live-migrates the High Priority VMs to healthy nodes.\\nLow Priority VMs are simply stopped/powered down to free up capacity for the critical workloads.", "type": "message", "user": "Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga"}
119c75ad-3bdc-40d4-9314-fe8889df0bbe	7639d894-7e95-4728-886a-b8278d551f38	1778228214.000174	Philip Jones	PJ - Build:\nMore investigation of Pure Storage Issue\nPulled into a call with SRE team who want to have a solution for silencing all alerts for maintenance windows or during cluster build / deployments.\nJoined a called regarding new cluster hk307. Three nodes not joining the cluster correctly. One registered as a node, but showing as not available. Two are just awol. I couldn't find any issues with the nodes or config, the HK networking team are claiming no issues there side either, but I think we need to get on a call with them to resolve. I've also adding the toolbox container to oc-mirror so we can run more troubleshooting on nodes as required.\nAttended a call (along with Raffa) with Dell to discuss CSM Operator issues. The call was positive and they've acknowledged some problems with the existing operator. Hopefully future operator versions will work out of the box (so to speak).\n(edited)	{"ts": "1778228214.000174", "text": "PJ - Build:\\nMore investigation of Pure Storage Issue\\nPulled into a call with SRE team who want to have a solution for silencing all alerts for maintenance windows or during cluster build / deployments.\\nJoined a called regarding new cluster hk307. Three nodes not joining the cluster correctly. One registered as a node, but showing as not available. Two are just awol. I couldn't find any issues with the nodes or config, the HK networking team are claiming no issues there side either, but I think we need to get on a call with them to resolve. I've also adding the toolbox container to oc-mirror so we can run more troubleshooting on nodes as required.\\nAttended a call (along with Raffa) with Dell to discuss CSM Operator issues. The call was positive and they've acknowledged some problems with the existing operator. Hopefully future operator versions will work out of the box (so to speak).\\n(edited)", "type": "message", "user": "Philip Jones"}
