a31ec882-53f1-45c9-8156-1a66efe5334d	920652ee-07ac-4596-bbba-dac90e7f07e4	1778212854.000234	James Force	@Rydekull - quick one for you, what's the position on removing kubeadmin? they want to do it, but just curious if its come up via Natti and you and what your view is?	{"ts": "1778212854.000234", "text": "@Rydekull - quick one for you, what's the position on removing kubeadmin? they want to do it, but just curious if its come up via Natti and you and what your view is?", "type": "message", "user": "James Force"}
6336aed8-5998-4ae2-94f5-d4a6173c963d	5e6ddf22-45d4-4d14-b038-13edd1c3a9eb	1778227799.000239	Petros Petrou	[Virtualization Migration Update]\nCustomer complains about not being able to migrate floppy disk drives!!! It is more than 30 years ago since I used a floppy but I had to dig further why do we need one at 2026. VMware ships .flp disk images which contain the pvscsi drivers for windows installation. The drivers are not included in the Windows ISO so they have to be somehow available during installation. OCPVirt does not need these drivers so issue resolved.\nMigration Parallelism\nThe customer has managed to migrate 70 vms in parallel but by using 70 individual mtv plans each containing 1 vm.\nI have captured output of one migration in AAP and will inspect the timings of each phase.\nNeed to consider whether doing parallel pre/post checks will add any benefit in terms of time saving. e.g. Are we saving 10mins, 30 mins, 1 hr? Can we live with without parallelism and do the checks sequentially?\nNeed to evaluate if the complexity of adding parallelism to the implementation could introduce more issues than the time saving benefit. e.g. We could have saved 1hr, but in some cases all the pre-checks are stucked somewhere and it is hard to continue the migration or troubleshoot!	{"ts": "1778227799.000239", "text": "[Virtualization Migration Update]\\nCustomer complains about not being able to migrate floppy disk drives!!! It is more than 30 years ago since I used a floppy but I had to dig further why do we need one at 2026. VMware ships .flp disk images which contain the pvscsi drivers for windows installation. The drivers are not included in the Windows ISO so they have to be somehow available during installation. OCPVirt does not need these drivers so issue resolved.\\nMigration Parallelism\\nThe customer has managed to migrate 70 vms in parallel but by using 70 individual mtv plans each containing 1 vm.\\nI have captured output of one migration in AAP and will inspect the timings of each phase.\\nNeed to consider whether doing parallel pre/post checks will add any benefit in terms of time saving. e.g. Are we saving 10mins, 30 mins, 1 hr? Can we live with without parallelism and do the checks sequentially?\\nNeed to evaluate if the complexity of adding parallelism to the implementation could introduce more issues than the time saving benefit. e.g. We could have saved 1hr, but in some cases all the pre-checks are stucked somewhere and it is hard to continue the migration or troubleshoot!", "type": "message", "user": "Petros Petrou"}
d0c0ae3f-24ab-476f-9592-ea5710dc261e	dd588b43-46a8-4961-b433-33c659f72378	1778237700.000240	Jens Feind	[VM Migration Debugging Session]\nToday Martin Necas from engineering joined us to debug a variety of issues.\nForemost the topic around migrating VMs with Shared Disks not take over their IP:\nSuccessful workaround applied to the customer environment by making sure the VM is actually running before migration so the forklift controller can catch its metadata (this is done for all the VMs of the targeted VSphere), it stays in in-memory cache before the controller or its pod restarts (current caveat), a verbose warning is to be implemented to make the user aware of this process pattern.\nWe migrated the VMs with their IPs preserved then. :check:\nNext, a constant pita was solved by a new flag in the Migration Plans:\nspec.rdmAsLun=true\nThats really great as it was something important pending at the customer, as they do not need change the disks any longer manually, or by a post-hook. :check:\nAlso, what is currently ongoing is that qemu-guest-agent sometimes not installs on Windows Servers, we gathered logs which have some hot leads to be followed up by engineering. :gear:\nCase: https://access.redhat.com/support/cases/#/case/04432578\nRegarding the VMware Tools Cleanup, there is a much more smooth and stable option on the horizon now, its an early dev-preview and therefore no where documented so far, but with 2.11.4 we can enable this featureGate in MTV:\noc patch ForkliftController  -n openshift-mtv forklift-controller  --type=merge -p '{"spec": {"feature_vsphere_vmware_driver_removal": "true"}}'\nIt contains most of the famous VMware removal script from Github including other findings from Red Hat to cleanup the VMs. Sudhir and the team agreed to start testing it with the ultimate goals of removing all the post-hook removal scripts currently scrubbing VMware Tools. :check:\nRegarding the Windows Failover Cluster issue to initialize and consume the shared disks we are still on going, with quite a range of potential error sources. Martin will now take it as priority and investigate in our RH labs. :wrench:\nAt this point I want to highlight Martins amazing dedication to our HSBC project! He stayed with us in a marathon call for many hours answering and tackling wide range of customer questions until 11:00 pm at night in his timezone. Customer team was really impressed and pleased by that. If you got a few reward zone points to spare, Martin definitely deserves it :slightly_smiling_face: :+1: (edited)	{"ts": "1778237700.000240", "text": "[VM Migration Debugging Session]\\nToday Martin Necas from engineering joined us to debug a variety of issues.\\nForemost the topic around migrating VMs with Shared Disks not take over their IP:\\nSuccessful workaround applied to the customer environment by making sure the VM is actually running before migration so the forklift controller can catch its metadata (this is done for all the VMs of the targeted VSphere), it stays in in-memory cache before the controller or its pod restarts (current caveat), a verbose warning is to be implemented to make the user aware of this process pattern.\\nWe migrated the VMs with their IPs preserved then. :check:\\nNext, a constant pita was solved by a new flag in the Migration Plans:\\nspec.rdmAsLun=true\\nThats really great as it was something important pending at the customer, as they do not need change the disks any longer manually, or by a post-hook. :check:\\nAlso, what is currently ongoing is that qemu-guest-agent sometimes not installs on Windows Servers, we gathered logs which have some hot leads to be followed up by engineering. :gear:\\nCase: https://access.redhat.com/support/cases/#/case/04432578\\nRegarding the VMware Tools Cleanup, there is a much more smooth and stable option on the horizon now, its an early dev-preview and therefore no where documented so far, but with 2.11.4 we can enable this featureGate in MTV:\\noc patch ForkliftController  -n openshift-mtv forklift-controller  --type=merge -p '{\\"spec\\": {\\"feature_vsphere_vmware_driver_removal\\": \\"true\\"}}'\\nIt contains most of the famous VMware removal script from Github including other findings from Red Hat to cleanup the VMs. Sudhir and the team agreed to start testing it with the ultimate goals of removing all the post-hook removal scripts currently scrubbing VMware Tools. :check:\\nRegarding the Windows Failover Cluster issue to initialize and consume the shared disks we are still on going, with quite a range of potential error sources. Martin will now take it as priority and investigate in our RH labs. :wrench:\\nAt this point I want to highlight Martins amazing dedication to our HSBC project! He stayed with us in a marathon call for many hours answering and tackling wide range of customer questions until 11:00 pm at night in his timezone. Customer team was really impressed and pleased by that. If you got a few reward zone points to spare, Martin definitely deserves it :slightly_smiling_face: :+1: (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "Jens Feind"}
