65564e50-f7fb-4a21-8259-02c3b04624fc	f406aa46-e1d2-4c70-b872-39234e1d7792	7777702900.000000	U_RAFFAE_1166B4A9	[Bulk Migration Testing with AAP]: Latency & AAP Platform: Magnus confirmed that a latency of around 400ms is acceptable, meaning no architectural changes to the AAP platform are required right now. We have also increased the UK database CPU to 18 and applied Red Hat’s fine-tuning recommendations.\n\nMigration SLAs & Strategy: The acceptable duration for VM migrations is set between 45 minutes and 1 hour 15 minutes. Our target split is 80% via storage offloading and 20% via cold migration.\n\nResource Consumption Testing: Because the Hong Kong lab is still under construction, Nitesh and Abhishek will conduct the next round of testing in the UK environment. We need to measure exact CPU, memory, and disk consumption during both Linux and Windows migrations to accurately calculate capacity needs.\n\nFuture Re-IP Scenarios: @janine told that future migrations requiring IP changes will involve additional pre- and post-migration automation tasks, taking longer. We will measure both standard and re-IP migration patterns and add safety buffers for unexpected scenarios.\n\nRecent Test Results: Abhishek confirmed that the recent 52 migrations had some failures, but they were not Ansible-related. The team is fixing these issues and will avoid problematic VM scenarios in the upcoming tests.	{"ts": "7777702900.000000", "text": "[Bulk Migration Testing with AAP]: Latency & AAP Platform: Magnus confirmed that a latency of around 400ms is acceptable, meaning no architectural changes to the AAP platform are required right now. We have also increased the UK database CPU to 18 and applied Red Hat’s fine-tuning recommendations.\\n\\nMigration SLAs & Strategy: The acceptable duration for VM migrations is set between 45 minutes and 1 hour 15 minutes. Our target split is 80% via storage offloading and 20% via cold migration.\\n\\nResource Consumption Testing: Because the Hong Kong lab is still under construction, Nitesh and Abhishek will conduct the next round of testing in the UK environment. We need to measure exact CPU, memory, and disk consumption during both Linux and Windows migrations to accurately calculate capacity needs.\\n\\nFuture Re-IP Scenarios: @janine told that future migrations requiring IP changes will involve additional pre- and post-migration automation tasks, taking longer. We will measure both standard and re-IP migration patterns and add safety buffers for unexpected scenarios.\\n\\nRecent Test Results: Abhishek confirmed that the recent 52 migrations had some failures, but they were not Ansible-related. The team is fixing these issues and will avoid problematic VM scenarios in the upcoming tests.", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_1166B4A9"}
70ad830d-7d4d-4a65-8bac-a3b532c0a7a0	3951b76b-ec38-4c80-bb30-706ecdf9315d	7777703000.000000	U_SEBAST_AC383055	[Labels - Data Architecture - Affinity and Anti-Affinity Discussions]:\n\nPart 1: The Aspirational Design (Gareth & Michele)\nWe successfully validated a strategy to model our physical failure domains in OpenShift to enable High Availability via Affinity/Anti-Affinity rules. This relies on tagging OpenShift nodes and VMs with three critical dimensions: Country, Data Center, and Fabric.\n\nWe mapped out exactly how to inject these labels across three journeys using Unicorn as the primary System of Record:\n\nCluster Build (GitOps): Querying Unicorn via bare-metal hostnames to label OpenShift nodes.\nVM Migration (MIOX): Pulling metadata to inject labels into the VM manifests during migration.\nNew VM Provisioning (Ansible/ITSP): Having Ansible query Unicorn to label net-new VMs at creation.\n\nPart 2: The Reality Check (Rushi)\n\nWhen I reviewed this with Rushi, he rightly pointed out that while this looks great on paper, it is highly aspirational for our day-to-day operations.\nThe Bottleneck: Strict Affinity/Anti-Affinity requires heavy coordination with Application Owners to define these rules properly.\nThe Operational Reality: In the event of an outage, VM Admins are solely focused on restoring services immediately. They will not have the time to debug why a VM isn’t scheduling due to Anti-Affinity constraints.\n\nPart 3: The Alternative Proposal (Service Classes)\n\nTo solve this, Rushi proposed a much more pragmatic, DR-focused approach: Service Classes (Priority Levels).\nInstead of forcing App Owners to define complex affinity rules, we simply ask them to define the priority of their service.\nIn the event of a massive failure (e.g., node, rack, or hall), the platform aggressively evacuates and live-migrates the High Priority VMs to healthy nodes.\nLow Priority VMs are simply stopped/powered down to free up capacity for the critical workloads.Philip Jones  [6:14 PM]\nPJ - Build:\n\nMore investigation of Pure Storage Issue\nPulled into a call with SRE team who want to have a solution for silencing all alerts for maintenance windows or during cluster build / deployments.\nJoined a called regarding new cluster hk307. Three nodes not joining the cluster correctly. One registered as a node, but showing as not available. Two are just awol. I couldn't find any issues with the nodes or config, the HK networking team are claiming no issues there side either, but I think we need to get on a call with them to resolve. I've also adding the toolbox container to oc-mirror so we can run more troubleshooting on nodes as required.\nAttended a call (along with Raffa) with Dell to discuss CSM Operator issues. The call was positive and they've acknowledged some problems with the existing operator. Hopefully future operator versions will work out of the box (so to speak).\n(edited)	{"ts": "7777703000.000000", "text": "[Labels - Data Architecture - Affinity and Anti-Affinity Discussions]:\\n\\nPart 1: The Aspirational Design (Gareth & Michele)\\nWe successfully validated a strategy to model our physical failure domains in OpenShift to enable High Availability via Affinity/Anti-Affinity rules. This relies on tagging OpenShift nodes and VMs with three critical dimensions: Country, Data Center, and Fabric.\\n\\nWe mapped out exactly how to inject these labels across three journeys using Unicorn as the primary System of Record:\\n\\nCluster Build (GitOps): Querying Unicorn via bare-metal hostnames to label OpenShift nodes.\\nVM Migration (MIOX): Pulling metadata to inject labels into the VM manifests during migration.\\nNew VM Provisioning (Ansible/ITSP): Having Ansible query Unicorn to label net-new VMs at creation.\\n\\nPart 2: The Reality Check (Rushi)\\n\\nWhen I reviewed this with Rushi, he rightly pointed out that while this looks great on paper, it is highly aspirational for our day-to-day operations.\\nThe Bottleneck: Strict Affinity/Anti-Affinity requires heavy coordination with Application Owners to define these rules properly.\\nThe Operational Reality: In the event of an outage, VM Admins are solely focused on restoring services immediately. They will not have the time to debug why a VM isn’t scheduling due to Anti-Affinity constraints.\\n\\nPart 3: The Alternative Proposal (Service Classes)\\n\\nTo solve this, Rushi proposed a much more pragmatic, DR-focused approach: Service Classes (Priority Levels).\\nInstead of forcing App Owners to define complex affinity rules, we simply ask them to define the priority of their service.\\nIn the event of a massive failure (e.g., node, rack, or hall), the platform aggressively evacuates and live-migrates the High Priority VMs to healthy nodes.\\nLow Priority VMs are simply stopped/powered down to free up capacity for the critical workloads.Philip Jones  [6:14 PM]\\nPJ - Build:\\n\\nMore investigation of Pure Storage Issue\\nPulled into a call with SRE team who want to have a solution for silencing all alerts for maintenance windows or during cluster build / deployments.\\nJoined a called regarding new cluster hk307. Three nodes not joining the cluster correctly. One registered as a node, but showing as not available. Two are just awol. I couldn't find any issues with the nodes or config, the HK networking team are claiming no issues there side either, but I think we need to get on a call with them to resolve. I've also adding the toolbox container to oc-mirror so we can run more troubleshooting on nodes as required.\\nAttended a call (along with Raffa) with Dell to discuss CSM Operator issues. The call was positive and they've acknowledged some problems with the existing operator. Hopefully future operator versions will work out of the box (so to speak).\\n(edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_SEBAST_AC383055"}
673e5a4d-a871-4c45-8491-de6a6adcb786	6fe29a5e-fa91-441e-805c-b6bf2d086e67	7777703100.000000	U_JAMES_F3856179	[build]\n\ntroubleshooted the pure issues making progress root cause, session with pure on monday\nspent the majority of my time today time with Suhas and Sourav on going over the remaining custom compliance operator rules, the final remaing things are not from the CIS KubeVirt benchmarks but custom HSBC AI invented ones so some not relevant/unclear, but think we're close to wrapping this piece of work up\nwent over next steps for the remote worker piece with Vinayak - it's a bit slow going but the priority on this has seemed to get lower so somewhat expected\nclarified the kubeletconfig changes required for the SRE team ... again, but the point/approach has stuck now as they've raised a PR for it now\nbonus: there is a bit of traction again on getting LACP link aggregation metrics for node exporter after prompting it... fingers crossed as this would be a nice addition and with a prom alert this could be quite informative and useful https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/issues/1604#issuecomment-4299123571 \n(edited)	{"ts": "7777703100.000000", "text": "[build]\\n\\ntroubleshooted the pure issues making progress root cause, session with pure on monday\\nspent the majority of my time today time with Suhas and Sourav on going over the remaining custom compliance operator rules, the final remaing things are not from the CIS KubeVirt benchmarks but custom HSBC AI invented ones so some not relevant/unclear, but think we're close to wrapping this piece of work up\\nwent over next steps for the remote worker piece with Vinayak - it's a bit slow going but the priority on this has seemed to get lower so somewhat expected\\nclarified the kubeletconfig changes required for the SRE team ... again, but the point/approach has stuck now as they've raised a PR for it now\\nbonus: there is a bit of traction again on getting LACP link aggregation metrics for node exporter after prompting it... fingers crossed as this would be a nice addition and with a prom alert this could be quite informative and useful https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/issues/1604#issuecomment-4299123571 \\n(edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179"}
fc9abe2e-1829-4c2f-9aef-1153e14e6f54	6fe29a5e-fa91-441e-805c-b6bf2d086e67	7777703101.000000	U_JAMES_F3856179	[build]\n\nSpoke with Mike on the LACP issue we came accross recently, they will add some checks on their side to mitigate this, but  he was curious if there was anything we can do on our side, I mentioned the lack of metrics in this area but he seemed happy about the upstream conversation and was curious to know if something did get added to node_exporter upstream when would that land in openshift\nQuick call on the ACM prorogation policies and then created an issue on the backlog to attempt to remove dependency on ACM after the initial seed... I will try to pick this up when I get some time\nRuishi dragged me into a call to discuss security policies and then went around the circles of gatekeeper, kyverno VAPs etc..  I circ led it back to the point of that VAPs were decided and work was already started here,,, I think there is a clear need for a proper backlog and planning of work as things feel a bit all over the place \nRushi called me again on the gitops approach and his want to move to something completely different (this binary artefact approach). I told him again this won't work, but he wants to allign with some 'deploycontol' approaches of their... @janine I've pinged you on the links in the teams chat.. maybe can you help me here? I think we just need to educate here on our controls for gitops and route to live process. IIRC you were working on something around this area?\nMTU... all our clusters MTU is just at 1400 (default)... this is probably a driver in the why live migrations are 'slow'....  @Rydekull I your help tomorrow on this to understand your last comment on the jump frames GH issue on upping this value to 9000 \nPure upgrade troubleshooting \nSnapshot issue (102) - I've just sent the operator log to pure for 102 on the pending failed upgrade due to snapshots... hopefully they can script up something to address that ... however.... if their approach is to manually delete records out of etcd using etcdctl... not sure I really like that, doesn't exactly feel elegant!\nVolume mapping (104)  - unfortunately the horrible idea I had the other day to manually remap volumes looks like it is a reality and the only way forward... albeit they provided us with a more elegant way to get the required info we need, and also let us know we don't need to define the targets in the purevolume resource	{"ts": "7777703101.000000", "text": "[build]\\n\\nSpoke with Mike on the LACP issue we came accross recently, they will add some checks on their side to mitigate this, but  he was curious if there was anything we can do on our side, I mentioned the lack of metrics in this area but he seemed happy about the upstream conversation and was curious to know if something did get added to node_exporter upstream when would that land in openshift\\nQuick call on the ACM prorogation policies and then created an issue on the backlog to attempt to remove dependency on ACM after the initial seed... I will try to pick this up when I get some time\\nRuishi dragged me into a call to discuss security policies and then went around the circles of gatekeeper, kyverno VAPs etc..  I circ led it back to the point of that VAPs were decided and work was already started here,,, I think there is a clear need for a proper backlog and planning of work as things feel a bit all over the place \\nRushi called me again on the gitops approach and his want to move to something completely different (this binary artefact approach). I told him again this won't work, but he wants to allign with some 'deploycontol' approaches of their... @janine I've pinged you on the links in the teams chat.. maybe can you help me here? I think we just need to educate here on our controls for gitops and route to live process. IIRC you were working on something around this area?\\nMTU... all our clusters MTU is just at 1400 (default)... this is probably a driver in the why live migrations are 'slow'....  @Rydekull I your help tomorrow on this to understand your last comment on the jump frames GH issue on upping this value to 9000 \\nPure upgrade troubleshooting \\nSnapshot issue (102) - I've just sent the operator log to pure for 102 on the pending failed upgrade due to snapshots... hopefully they can script up something to address that ... however.... if their approach is to manually delete records out of etcd using etcdctl... not sure I really like that, doesn't exactly feel elegant!\\nVolume mapping (104)  - unfortunately the horrible idea I had the other day to manually remap volumes looks like it is a reality and the only way forward... albeit they provided us with a more elegant way to get the required info we need, and also let us know we don't need to define the targets in the purevolume resource", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179", "thread_ts": "7777703100.000000"}
f7bd1ca6-a461-4146-8ad5-40368c426d7a	984fa56e-8e77-4cd6-961c-4f651d4e55d9	7777703200.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[node drain] Balamurugan is saying that draining nodes takes so long that the VMs eventually fail to live migrate and they have to be manually moved. This should not be happening even if storage is slow. I asked him to open a case and to involve @Shebi . @James Force you are also in that conversation so perhaps you can add more color.	{"ts": "7777703200.000000", "text": "[node drain] Balamurugan is saying that draining nodes takes so long that the VMs eventually fail to live migrate and they have to be manually moved. This should not be happening even if storage is slow. I asked him to open a case and to involve @Shebi . @James Force you are also in that conversation so perhaps you can add more color.", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
867bdf7e-cb85-4bb3-93df-d6259e66f14b	3367cc56-c0c1-4057-85f8-fc2534d979ff	7777703300.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[architecture documentation] with regards to this thread https://redhat-internal.slack.com/archives/C099TDJDDKL/p1776434503713569 , I hope everyone is making progress. Here is my update:\n@janine has been helping me creating teh documentation that was assigned by Vijay to Michele, we should be in decent spot enough to keep Vijay happy. @janine will keep working with Michele in my absence.\n@Carina Almeida please keep people accountable to complete their documentation. the deadline is Friday 5/1\n\nPS: I have been in many projects in which red hat has been in defect because we didn't deliver the documentation. Let's make sure this is not one of those.\n[arch documentation] we need to finish the following pieces of architecture docs in two weeks. Please let's sure we deliver on this, as red hatter we are notoriously slow/bad at this part..:\n\ncompute design for non-hic, status: empty, assigned: Michele, Raf and now @janine \nsecurity: status: Natty populated it: @Rydekull to review, lean and filter. This is the piece that concerns Vijay\ndata architecture (i.e. labels and annotation): populated by Gareth. @Shebi to review and approve. @Shebi since you are also following teh migration track, let';s make sure this guidance is implemented. I have not seen those labels on the migrated VMs. I could be wrong though.\nTertiary Vault: sta… \nThread in proj-eos-mvp | Apr 17th | View messageRaffaele Spazzoli  [3:38 PM]\n[bsod] this is teh list of well-knon bsod scenarios @Shebi @pdelashe @Petros please share this with the customer so they can build detection rules in the pre-flight automation.	{"ts": "7777703300.000000", "text": "[architecture documentation] with regards to this thread https://redhat-internal.slack.com/archives/C099TDJDDKL/p1776434503713569 , I hope everyone is making progress. Here is my update:\\n@janine has been helping me creating teh documentation that was assigned by Vijay to Michele, we should be in decent spot enough to keep Vijay happy. @janine will keep working with Michele in my absence.\\n@Carina Almeida please keep people accountable to complete their documentation. the deadline is Friday 5/1\\n\\nPS: I have been in many projects in which red hat has been in defect because we didn't deliver the documentation. Let's make sure this is not one of those.\\n[arch documentation] we need to finish the following pieces of architecture docs in two weeks. Please let's sure we deliver on this, as red hatter we are notoriously slow/bad at this part..:\\n\\ncompute design for non-hic, status: empty, assigned: Michele, Raf and now @janine \\nsecurity: status: Natty populated it: @Rydekull to review, lean and filter. This is the piece that concerns Vijay\\ndata architecture (i.e. labels and annotation): populated by Gareth. @Shebi to review and approve. @Shebi since you are also following teh migration track, let';s make sure this guidance is implemented. I have not seen those labels on the migrated VMs. I could be wrong though.\\nTertiary Vault: sta… \\nThread in proj-eos-mvp | Apr 17th | View messageRaffaele Spazzoli  [3:38 PM]\\n[bsod] this is teh list of well-knon bsod scenarios @Shebi @pdelashe @Petros please share this with the customer so they can build detection rules in the pre-flight automation.", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
2a8bff5c-84d4-4607-abed-94abc6d01b44	2e590429-30fb-4e0a-b152-224cd582f105	7777705400.000000	U_PETROS_2EB3D870	[Virtualization Migration Update]\n\n\nCustomer complains about not being able to migrate floppy disk drives!!! It is more than 30 years ago since I used a floppy but I had to dig further why do we need one at 2026. VMware ships .flp disk images which contain the pvscsi drivers for windows installation. The drivers are not included in the Windows ISO so they have to be somehow available during installation. OCPVirt does not need these drivers so issue resolved.\nMigration Parallelism\nThe customer has managed to migrate 70 vms in parallel but by using 70 individual mtv plans each containing 1 vm.\nI have captured output of one migration in AAP and will inspect the timings of each phase.\nNeed to consider whether doing parallel pre/post checks will add any benefit in terms of time saving. e.g. Are we saving 10mins, 30 mins, 1 hr? Can we live with without parallelism and do the checks sequentially?\nNeed to evaluate if the complexity of adding parallelism to the implementation could introduce more issues than the time saving benefit. e.g. We could have saved 1hr, but in some cases all the pre-checks are stucked somewhere and it is hard to con[8:51 PM]\n[VM Migration Debugging Session]\nToday Martin Necas from engineering joined us to debug a variety of issues.\n\nForemost the topic around migrating VMs with Shared Disks not take over their IP:\nSuccessful workaround applied to the customer environment by making sure the VM is actually running before migration so the forklift controller can catch its metadata (this is done for all the VMs of the targeted VSphere), it stays in in-memory cache before the controller or its pod restarts (current caveat), a verbose warning is to be implemented to make the user aware of this process pattern.\nWe migrated the VMs with their IPs preserved then. :check:\n\n\n\nNext, a constant pita was solved by a new flag in the Migration Plans:\nspec.rdmAsLun=true\nThats really great as it was something important pending at the customer, as they do not need change the disks any longer manually, or by a post-hook. :check:\n\nAlso, what is currently ongoing is that qemu-guest-agent sometimes not installs on Windows Servers, we gathered logs which have some hot leads to be followed up by engineering. :gear:\nCase: https://access.redhat.com/support/cases/#/case/04432578\n\nRegarding the VMware Tools Cleanup, there is a much more smooth and stable option on the horizon now, its an early dev-preview and therefore no where documented so far, but with 2.11.4 we can enable this featureGate in MTV:\noc patch ForkliftController  -n openshift-mtv forklift-controller  --type=merge -p '{"spec": {"feature_vsphere_vmware_driver_removal": "true"}}'\nIt contains most of the famous VMware removal script from Github including other findings from Red Hat to cleanup the VMs. Sudhir and the team agreed to start testing it with the ultimate goals of removing all the post-hook removal scripts currently scrubbing VMware Tools. :check:\n\nRegarding the Windows Failover Cluster issue to initialize and consume the shared disks we are still on going, with quite a range of potential error sources. Martin will now take it as priority and investigate in our RH labs. :wrench:\nAt this point I want to highlight Martins amazing dedication to our HSBC project! He stayed with us in a marathon call for many hours answering and tackling wide range of customer questions until 11:00 pm at night in his timezone. Customer team was really impressed and pleased by that. If you got a few reward zone points to spare, Martin definitely deserves it :slightly_smiling_face: :+1: (edited)	{"ts": "7777705400.000000", "text": "[Virtualization Migration Update]\\n\\n\\nCustomer complains about not being able to migrate floppy disk drives!!! It is more than 30 years ago since I used a floppy but I had to dig further why do we need one at 2026. VMware ships .flp disk images which contain the pvscsi drivers for windows installation. The drivers are not included in the Windows ISO so they have to be somehow available during installation. OCPVirt does not need these drivers so issue resolved.\\nMigration Parallelism\\nThe customer has managed to migrate 70 vms in parallel but by using 70 individual mtv plans each containing 1 vm.\\nI have captured output of one migration in AAP and will inspect the timings of each phase.\\nNeed to consider whether doing parallel pre/post checks will add any benefit in terms of time saving. e.g. Are we saving 10mins, 30 mins, 1 hr? Can we live with without parallelism and do the checks sequentially?\\nNeed to evaluate if the complexity of adding parallelism to the implementation could introduce more issues than the time saving benefit. e.g. We could have saved 1hr, but in some cases all the pre-checks are stucked somewhere and it is hard to con[8:51 PM]\\n[VM Migration Debugging Session]\\nToday Martin Necas from engineering joined us to debug a variety of issues.\\n\\nForemost the topic around migrating VMs with Shared Disks not take over their IP:\\nSuccessful workaround applied to the customer environment by making sure the VM is actually running before migration so the forklift controller can catch its metadata (this is done for all the VMs of the targeted VSphere), it stays in in-memory cache before the controller or its pod restarts (current caveat), a verbose warning is to be implemented to make the user aware of this process pattern.\\nWe migrated the VMs with their IPs preserved then. :check:\\n\\n\\n\\nNext, a constant pita was solved by a new flag in the Migration Plans:\\nspec.rdmAsLun=true\\nThats really great as it was something important pending at the customer, as they do not need change the disks any longer manually, or by a post-hook. :check:\\n\\nAlso, what is currently ongoing is that qemu-guest-agent sometimes not installs on Windows Servers, we gathered logs which have some hot leads to be followed up by engineering. :gear:\\nCase: https://access.redhat.com/support/cases/#/case/04432578\\n\\nRegarding the VMware Tools Cleanup, there is a much more smooth and stable option on the horizon now, its an early dev-preview and therefore no where documented so far, but with 2.11.4 we can enable this featureGate in MTV:\\noc patch ForkliftController  -n openshift-mtv forklift-controller  --type=merge -p '{\\"spec\\": {\\"feature_vsphere_vmware_driver_removal\\": \\"true\\"}}'\\nIt contains most of the famous VMware removal script from Github including other findings from Red Hat to cleanup the VMs. Sudhir and the team agreed to start testing it with the ultimate goals of removing all the post-hook removal scripts currently scrubbing VMware Tools. :check:\\n\\nRegarding the Windows Failover Cluster issue to initialize and consume the shared disks we are still on going, with quite a range of potential error sources. Martin will now take it as priority and investigate in our RH labs. :wrench:\\nAt this point I want to highlight Martins amazing dedication to our HSBC project! He stayed with us in a marathon call for many hours answering and tackling wide range of customer questions until 11:00 pm at night in his timezone. Customer team was really impressed and pleased by that. If you got a few reward zone points to spare, Martin definitely deserves it :slightly_smiling_face: :+1: (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_PETROS_2EB3D870"}
c0d2fde8-395d-4e3e-bf61-3393e8162246	2460278b-3c08-40b6-a5f7-6a2336b3a73b	7777715500.000000	U_ALEXAN_D03599F6	[New Security contact for from the BAU Cyber engineering, OpenShift for the Infrastructure Security team] Marcin Fornal will be the operational side of cybersec as far I understand it. He works with the Linux / Unix cybersecurity engineering team in the bank.	{"ts": "7777715500.000000", "text": "[New Security contact for from the BAU Cyber engineering, OpenShift for the Infrastructure Security team] Marcin Fornal will be the operational side of cybersec as far I understand it. He works with the Linux / Unix cybersecurity engineering team in the bank.", "type": "message", "user": "U_ALEXAN_D03599F6"}
3938456d-4952-4784-940b-3f0c6051b9b7	0cc0a65d-b706-4ca8-be41-ffc708bbcfb2	7777703400.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[acm secret propagation] @James Force there is a meeting on Monday on this, I added you to it so you can explain the mechanism. from what I can see from the gitops repo, we propagate only the certs and the github credentials. in both instance we should find a way to cut the cordon from ACM and have argocd pull its own secrets after the initial seed. I have an idea on how to do it based on the same mechanism on how we can set the root app to not auto-sync. cc @Shebi .	{"ts": "7777703400.000000", "text": "[acm secret propagation] @James Force there is a meeting on Monday on this, I added you to it so you can explain the mechanism. from what I can see from the gitops repo, we propagate only the certs and the github credentials. in both instance we should find a way to cut the cordon from ACM and have argocd pull its own secrets after the initial seed. I have an idea on how to do it based on the same mechanism on how we can set the root app to not auto-sync. cc @Shebi .", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
5caffcc4-0875-4acc-b81f-702366b2abec	da6a8302-18fd-465a-a89b-002ee667c367	7777703500.000000	U_PER_F4CE6A9D	[US/MX Workshops]: We have a request for the workshop to clarify our support engagement in both pilots (Test Migrations) and production migration (Volume Migrations). I need help from @Anne @Carina Almeida , @pdelashe, even @Nikos if you have any slides, ideas, workflows, screenshots, documents, share it them here and I will create a slide out of this. My understanding is the following about both process:\n\nPilot Migrations (Consulting-Led)\nDuring the pilot phase, Red Hat Consulting will be heavily involved in the execution and troubleshooting. The goal here is to establish our baseline and refine our automation.\n\nProactive Support Case: Created prior to execution.\nExecution: Consulting is involved in the communication structure of the migration (we are passive listeners here).\nFailure Handling (The New RCA Proposal): If a VM migration fails, the VM is rolled back in VMware. The failed OpenShift VM is then moved to an isolated “Quarantine/Triage” environment. (Note: This isolation environment is not currently in place; we will officially propose building this during the upcoming workshops).\nResolution: Consulting performs Root Cause Analysis (RCA) in the triage environment, finds the issue and ask for the fixes the underlying automation (Narender), and places the VM back into the backlog to be rescheduled for a future migration wave.\n\n\n2. Volume / Production Migrations (Support-Led)\n\nOnce we move into bulk production migrations, the execution and issue resolution will shift entirely to our Support organization.\n\nProactive Support Case: Created prior to execution to ensure global readiness.\nExecution & Failure Handling: Handled 100% by Red Hat Support. This will leverage our Technical Account Managers (TAMs) across the different timezones to ensure “follow-the-sun” coverage, alongside the recently onboarded ESS team.\nResolution: The Support and ESS teams will drive immediate break/fix resolutions and coordinate any necessary escalations directly.\n\n\nPlease review this logic and let me know your thoughts or if you see any gaps.	{"ts": "7777703500.000000", "text": "[US/MX Workshops]: We have a request for the workshop to clarify our support engagement in both pilots (Test Migrations) and production migration (Volume Migrations). I need help from @Anne @Carina Almeida , @pdelashe, even @Nikos if you have any slides, ideas, workflows, screenshots, documents, share it them here and I will create a slide out of this. My understanding is the following about both process:\\n\\nPilot Migrations (Consulting-Led)\\nDuring the pilot phase, Red Hat Consulting will be heavily involved in the execution and troubleshooting. The goal here is to establish our baseline and refine our automation.\\n\\nProactive Support Case: Created prior to execution.\\nExecution: Consulting is involved in the communication structure of the migration (we are passive listeners here).\\nFailure Handling (The New RCA Proposal): If a VM migration fails, the VM is rolled back in VMware. The failed OpenShift VM is then moved to an isolated “Quarantine/Triage” environment. (Note: This isolation environment is not currently in place; we will officially propose building this during the upcoming workshops).\\nResolution: Consulting performs Root Cause Analysis (RCA) in the triage environment, finds the issue and ask for the fixes the underlying automation (Narender), and places the VM back into the backlog to be rescheduled for a future migration wave.\\n\\n\\n2. Volume / Production Migrations (Support-Led)\\n\\nOnce we move into bulk production migrations, the execution and issue resolution will shift entirely to our Support organization.\\n\\nProactive Support Case: Created prior to execution to ensure global readiness.\\nExecution & Failure Handling: Handled 100% by Red Hat Support. This will leverage our Technical Account Managers (TAMs) across the different timezones to ensure “follow-the-sun” coverage, alongside the recently onboarded ESS team.\\nResolution: The Support and ESS teams will drive immediate break/fix resolutions and coordinate any necessary escalations directly.\\n\\n\\nPlease review this logic and let me know your thoughts or if you see any gaps.", "type": "message", "user": "U_PER_F4CE6A9D"}
72bd2b0f-a2cc-4de1-8bb9-8ad4aee2068f	bb764c70-5994-4373-87d8-237be290e7aa	7777703600.000000	U_ALEXAN_D03599F6	[GitHub & Nexus] just had a meeting with the owner of GitHub / Nexus iwht Michele, its more about how to get it out there across the world to all geo's / dcs. But also what happens if it goes offline, it was very hard to navigate the conversation without them being on the NDA, so Michele is going to get them on the NDA and then continue the dialogue.	{"ts": "7777703600.000000", "text": "[GitHub & Nexus] just had a meeting with the owner of GitHub / Nexus iwht Michele, its more about how to get it out there across the world to all geo's / dcs. But also what happens if it goes offline, it was very hard to navigate the conversation without them being on the NDA, so Michele is going to get them on the NDA and then continue the dialogue.", "type": "message", "user": "U_ALEXAN_D03599F6"}
