41bf22d7-9783-43e7-99d5-aac37826c697	d96b534a-4778-40c5-9296-b0cefaaf43de	7777709900.000000	U_PHILIP_740C6823	[Build: PJ]\n\nGood meeting to discuss the HSBC git control and commit strategy. \nContinued with documenting the new release and cluster upgrade processes.\n... 90% sure there won't be stretch clusters, but final decision is pending...\nSome queries raised from Cisco relating to customisations to OCP Virt. \nThey're asking if they could change OCP Virt to run virt handler pods to in host networking mode. This is to ensure that live migration traffic is sent via the management interface, now they're able to split traffic across multiple interfaces e.g. management bond and a VM bond. This is impacting their ability to offer live migrations with the current build.\n\nWe need to review the ACM cluster and ensure it can deploy new clusters with Cilium networking enabled.\nI will be away from Fri 22nd, returning Wed 27th and will need someone to cover the Isovalent calls in my absence.  @janine, are you okay to cover these days? Not sure we need to cover the recharge day?	{"ts": "7777709900.000000", "text": "[Build: PJ]\\n\\nGood meeting to discuss the HSBC git control and commit strategy. \\nContinued with documenting the new release and cluster upgrade processes.\\n... 90% sure there won't be stretch clusters, but final decision is pending...\\nSome queries raised from Cisco relating to customisations to OCP Virt. \\nThey're asking if they could change OCP Virt to run virt handler pods to in host networking mode. This is to ensure that live migration traffic is sent via the management interface, now they're able to split traffic across multiple interfaces e.g. management bond and a VM bond. This is impacting their ability to offer live migrations with the current build.\\n\\nWe need to review the ACM cluster and ensure it can deploy new clusters with Cilium networking enabled.\\nI will be away from Fri 22nd, returning Wed 27th and will need someone to cover the Isovalent calls in my absence.  @janine, are you okay to cover these days? Not sure we need to cover the recharge day?", "type": "message", "user": "U_PHILIP_740C6823"}
b80044b0-08eb-4e79-9394-91cb4077839b	2019a02f-eb80-46d9-96c6-39c5624e5c4d	7777710000.000000	U_SEBAST_AC383055	[Storage Offload overloading the storage]: On the migrations on Wednesday I saw many  migration-plan* pods on pending state, the error was\nO/22 nodes are available: pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims. preemption: 0/22 nodes are available: 22\nPreemption is not helpful for schedulingI am assuming we were not doing storage offload and all the migrations went to the regular storage migration path. Has anyone seen that before?\n2 files Carina Almeida  [10:49 AM]\n[Migration Bucket 1 ]\n\n- Bundle 3A - starting at 10am BST - 46 VMs\nMigration started at 10:01\n\nRH team - @Shebi @jfeind and I\nI'll ping you if / when needed :wink: (edited)	{"ts": "7777710000.000000", "text": "[Storage Offload overloading the storage]: On the migrations on Wednesday I saw many  migration-plan* pods on pending state, the error was\\nO/22 nodes are available: pod has unbound immediate PersistentVolumeClaims. preemption: 0/22 nodes are available: 22\\nPreemption is not helpful for schedulingI am assuming we were not doing storage offload and all the migrations went to the regular storage migration path. Has anyone seen that before?\\n2 files Carina Almeida  [10:49 AM]\\n[Migration Bucket 1 ]\\n\\n- Bundle 3A - starting at 10am BST - 46 VMs\\nMigration started at 10:01\\n\\nRH team - @Shebi @jfeind and I\\nI'll ping you if / when needed :wink: (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_SEBAST_AC383055"}
a2d69830-9d7e-49ab-96d3-360b4c066558	2c5b5159-3d48-4e51-813f-80dcf7d63bf6	7777710100.000000	U_JAMES_F3856179	[build/security etcd encryption]\nSo... Sourav has been working with security on all things compliance related and commvault etcd backups and.. it's seemingly been decided (by security) that etcd needs to be encrypted. I know we wanted to push doing this until 4.22 when that KMS integration comes in.. but seems they want to do something in the interim... so it looks like we'll have to turn on etcd encryption and the commvault backups will backup the db and keys to the same place (daft we know, but security was okay with this).. defiantly feels a box ticking exercise, but I'm not that opposed to push back on this too much as it does't really have any negative impact too much (just seems a little silly)... thoughts? @rspazzol @Rydekull @janine	{"ts": "7777710100.000000", "text": "[build/security etcd encryption]\\nSo... Sourav has been working with security on all things compliance related and commvault etcd backups and.. it's seemingly been decided (by security) that etcd needs to be encrypted. I know we wanted to push doing this until 4.22 when that KMS integration comes in.. but seems they want to do something in the interim... so it looks like we'll have to turn on etcd encryption and the commvault backups will backup the db and keys to the same place (daft we know, but security was okay with this).. defiantly feels a box ticking exercise, but I'm not that opposed to push back on this too much as it does't really have any negative impact too much (just seems a little silly)... thoughts? @rspazzol @Rydekull @janine", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179"}
b31a65c0-be2f-45e8-b58c-29ebe6f81a69	868cde2b-a169-44f5-8f81-8cd1d1e103e7	7777710200.000000	U_CARINA_D5667D0C	[Migration Bucket 1 ]\n\n- Bundle 3C - started at 1pm BST with 17 VMs	{"ts": "7777710200.000000", "text": "[Migration Bucket 1 ]\\n\\n- Bundle 3C - started at 1pm BST with 17 VMs", "type": "message", "user": "U_CARINA_D5667D0C"}
231c8d5a-98cb-44b1-9144-be119d94fa79	6f61d945-422e-4e7b-9e63-a3d21f2c8b3a	7777710300.000000	U_JAMES_F3856179	[build - nexus repos]\nTo date, every cluster has been built using the Nexus instance in the UK. We now have repo mirroring set up from the UK to the HK Nexus and Prerana and Shwetali have proposed using environment substitution for the Nexus endpoint for our clusters (i.e., nexus3.uk vs. nexus3.hk) see the image I'll attach for a draft PR Prerana raised. However, we would also need to add a Helm variable for this instance and modify the Helm charts to support it...as there isn't much use in building a cluster in HK that pulls from the UK only to switch repositories later. I'm not sure i'm a huge fan of this.. I was thinking of adding multiple mirrors (as they are supposedly ordered) as I think thats safer and it also gives us additional protection say if an image cant be pulled from 1 geo for some reason it can still be pulled from the other... thoughts? @rspazzol @janine @Philip Jones @Rydekull https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/openshift_container_platform/4.21/html/config_apis/imagedigestmirrorset-config-openshift-io-v1#spec[…]ors-2\ndocs.redhat.comChapter 15. ImageDigestMirrorSet [config.openshift.io/v1] | Config APIs | OpenShift Container Platform | 4.21 | Red Hat DocumentationChapter 15. ImageDigestMirrorSet [config.openshift.io/v1] | Config APIs | OpenShift Container Platform | 4.21 | Red Hat DocumentationJens Feind  [3:18 PM]\nThanks to everyone who helped pushing out MTV 2.11.6 fast into the Labs, this will help us move forward in both the Migration and Testing Workstreams :+1:	{"ts": "7777710300.000000", "text": "[build - nexus repos]\\nTo date, every cluster has been built using the Nexus instance in the UK. We now have repo mirroring set up from the UK to the HK Nexus and Prerana and Shwetali have proposed using environment substitution for the Nexus endpoint for our clusters (i.e., nexus3.uk vs. nexus3.hk) see the image I'll attach for a draft PR Prerana raised. However, we would also need to add a Helm variable for this instance and modify the Helm charts to support it...as there isn't much use in building a cluster in HK that pulls from the UK only to switch repositories later. I'm not sure i'm a huge fan of this.. I was thinking of adding multiple mirrors (as they are supposedly ordered) as I think thats safer and it also gives us additional protection say if an image cant be pulled from 1 geo for some reason it can still be pulled from the other... thoughts? @rspazzol @janine @Philip Jones @Rydekull https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/openshift_container_platform/4.21/html/config_apis/imagedigestmirrorset-config-openshift-io-v1#spec[…]ors-2\\ndocs.redhat.comChapter 15. ImageDigestMirrorSet [config.openshift.io/v1] | Config APIs | OpenShift Container Platform | 4.21 | Red Hat DocumentationChapter 15. ImageDigestMirrorSet [config.openshift.io/v1] | Config APIs | OpenShift Container Platform | 4.21 | Red Hat DocumentationJens Feind  [3:18 PM]\\nThanks to everyone who helped pushing out MTV 2.11.6 fast into the Labs, this will help us move forward in both the Migration and Testing Workstreams :+1:", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179"}
fb8df0e7-16cc-4a16-bb76-0c4242407bf2	9f34243b-5214-4dcf-9e6d-30099c118cd0	7777710400.000000	U_JAMES_F3856179	[build - node labelling]\nSo it seems that the build team have added a new app node-metadata-configuration to which will add labels to nodes... this is partly my fault as I suggested this approach for the remote worker test, but i didn't realise it'd be taken forward as an approach to the rest of the estate (especially after not being tested properly in the lab) :confused:  I've since informed them the best way to label up nodes which haven't yet been provisioned is to add the bmac.agent-install.openshift.io.node-label. annotation to the BMH resource.. but basically ... I think we have a bit of an issue now.. and I'm a bit stumped on how we elegantly handle day 2 labelling of nodes in a gitops manor. See attached image for what's been done for reference..... so this works and it has to be done through defining resources and not patches as kuztomise basically cannot patch something it doesn't 'own', so you have to define it thorough a resource... the issue here is that if the application is deleted node-metadata-configuration  then because we have pruning enabled in our root app it thus deletes the resources... so in this case it deletes the nodes.. hence potentially bloody destructive here.... so a couple questions:\n\n\nAnyone have any ideas on how to elegantly do day2 labelling via gitops? (day 1 is fine via that bmac BMH annotation)\nShould we turn off auto pruning in the root app? (it's on atm, and I suppose there are pros and cons for doing this)\n\n\nthoughts @rspazzol @Philip Jones and co?\nimage.png Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga  [5:01 PM]\n[VM Migration Updates (Today’s batches) ]: We processed a total of 92 VMs today across three batches. The majority of the migrations were successful, but we did hit a few consistent issues that we need to keep tracking.\n\nOverall Statistics:\n\n   * Success (Clean): 62 VMs\n    * Success (Fix Forward): 11 VMs (Primarily IP assignment/agent issues)\n    * Rollbacks: 19 VMs\n\nKey Failures:\n\nXFS / File System Corruption (10 VMs): This was the major driver for our Linux rollbacks today (e.g., gbl20127087, gbl20051434). This is an OS/filesystem issue that is not easily fixable from the MTV side. We are currently working closely with the customer to determine the best approach to handle these XFS corruption issues prior to migration. @rspazzol I am still not sure we can fix this without asking for additional downtime.\n\nVIB / Allocate Disks Issue (11 VMs): We saw several rollbacks due to the known VIB / storage populator issue. The good news is that most of these are addressable with the fixes in our latest MTV 2.11.6 release. We tested this now in Cluster 104 and it is working now, I would like to make this update on production ASAP @Carina Almeida can we make sure this is communicated so it takes more priority?\n\nMissing IP / Post-Migration (6 VMs): Several Windows VMs successfully migrated but required a “Fix Forward” because OpenShift couldn’t find an IP address or the OCP agent wasn’t running post-migration. We were able to push these through, but it adds manual overhead. I hope this is fixed updated in 2.11.6  or (11.3.6 :wink: )	{"ts": "7777710400.000000", "text": "[build - node labelling]\\nSo it seems that the build team have added a new app node-metadata-configuration to which will add labels to nodes... this is partly my fault as I suggested this approach for the remote worker test, but i didn't realise it'd be taken forward as an approach to the rest of the estate (especially after not being tested properly in the lab) :confused:  I've since informed them the best way to label up nodes which haven't yet been provisioned is to add the bmac.agent-install.openshift.io.node-label. annotation to the BMH resource.. but basically ... I think we have a bit of an issue now.. and I'm a bit stumped on how we elegantly handle day 2 labelling of nodes in a gitops manor. See attached image for what's been done for reference..... so this works and it has to be done through defining resources and not patches as kuztomise basically cannot patch something it doesn't 'own', so you have to define it thorough a resource... the issue here is that if the application is deleted node-metadata-configuration  then because we have pruning enabled in our root app it thus deletes the resources... so in this case it deletes the nodes.. hence potentially bloody destructive here.... so a couple questions:\\n\\n\\nAnyone have any ideas on how to elegantly do day2 labelling via gitops? (day 1 is fine via that bmac BMH annotation)\\nShould we turn off auto pruning in the root app? (it's on atm, and I suppose there are pros and cons for doing this)\\n\\n\\nthoughts @rspazzol @Philip Jones and co?\\nimage.png Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga  [5:01 PM]\\n[VM Migration Updates (Today’s batches) ]: We processed a total of 92 VMs today across three batches. The majority of the migrations were successful, but we did hit a few consistent issues that we need to keep tracking.\\n\\nOverall Statistics:\\n\\n   * Success (Clean): 62 VMs\\n    * Success (Fix Forward): 11 VMs (Primarily IP assignment/agent issues)\\n    * Rollbacks: 19 VMs\\n\\nKey Failures:\\n\\nXFS / File System Corruption (10 VMs): This was the major driver for our Linux rollbacks today (e.g., gbl20127087, gbl20051434). This is an OS/filesystem issue that is not easily fixable from the MTV side. We are currently working closely with the customer to determine the best approach to handle these XFS corruption issues prior to migration. @rspazzol I am still not sure we can fix this without asking for additional downtime.\\n\\nVIB / Allocate Disks Issue (11 VMs): We saw several rollbacks due to the known VIB / storage populator issue. The good news is that most of these are addressable with the fixes in our latest MTV 2.11.6 release. We tested this now in Cluster 104 and it is working now, I would like to make this update on production ASAP @Carina Almeida can we make sure this is communicated so it takes more priority?\\n\\nMissing IP / Post-Migration (6 VMs): Several Windows VMs successfully migrated but required a “Fix Forward” because OpenShift couldn’t find an IP address or the OCP agent wasn’t running post-migration. We were able to push these through, but it adds manual overhead. I hope this is fixed updated in 2.11.6  or (11.3.6 :wink: )", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179"}
97958fbe-1e47-444f-9b57-62cdf435e4fc	362cd99a-3756-49e0-bade-a8edaa000067	7777711000.000000	U_JENS_9E9831E8	[Database VM Performance Test]\nToday we had a session with HSBC (Devdatt, Sudhir and guests from the DB Team) aswell as Alex, Shebi and me,\nregarding the measured db performance gaps of VMs (RHEL 8.10 running Oracle DB) on the current Vsphere cluster in  the Lab and 104.\nWe got a clear picture of the current state (clusters, vms and tools used) as well as we plan to proceed. The topic will move from Sudhir (VM Migration/Automation) to Devdatt (Testing) and we decided on the following two testcases shouldering this:\nTestcase 1: Setup the Lab (1 VM on Vsphere and 1 on 104) make sure both are on the same storage array (DELL as this will be the main storage provider DBs are running on), make sure the tool which creates the synthetic data is in place and how to get the results visble.\nTestcase 2: Start tweaking (tuneD etc) the VM on OCP to measure and close the performance gap.Carina Almeida  [1:16 PM]\n@channel To all members of the migration squads\n\n Please note that if a PM requests your attendance on a migration call, this must take priority over all other activities.\nThese calls are critical for unblocking live migrations, so your prompt attendance is essential.\n:thank_you_smile:	{"ts": "7777711000.000000", "text": "[Database VM Performance Test]\\nToday we had a session with HSBC (Devdatt, Sudhir and guests from the DB Team) aswell as Alex, Shebi and me,\\nregarding the measured db performance gaps of VMs (RHEL 8.10 running Oracle DB) on the current Vsphere cluster in  the Lab and 104.\\nWe got a clear picture of the current state (clusters, vms and tools used) as well as we plan to proceed. The topic will move from Sudhir (VM Migration/Automation) to Devdatt (Testing) and we decided on the following two testcases shouldering this:\\nTestcase 1: Setup the Lab (1 VM on Vsphere and 1 on 104) make sure both are on the same storage array (DELL as this will be the main storage provider DBs are running on), make sure the tool which creates the synthetic data is in place and how to get the results visble.\\nTestcase 2: Start tweaking (tuneD etc) the VM on OCP to measure and close the performance gap.Carina Almeida  [1:16 PM]\\n@channel To all members of the migration squads\\n\\n Please note that if a PM requests your attendance on a migration call, this must take priority over all other activities.\\nThese calls are critical for unblocking live migrations, so your prompt attendance is essential.\\n:thank_you_smile:", "type": "message", "user": "U_JENS_9E9831E8"}
04f231aa-36a9-4204-bb0a-542b430b58b4	a6ef7530-895b-458b-a84b-b1f9f7308afc	7777717700.000000	U_ALEXAN_D03599F6	Anyone got the number to IT support readily available? :slightly_smiling_face:	{"ts": "7777717700.000000", "text": "Anyone got the number to IT support readily available? :slightly_smiling_face:", "type": "message", "user": "U_ALEXAN_D03599F6"}
33db95df-2a50-498e-8d7f-f35145c4e5f3	0ab7ab44-dbf4-4327-a8f0-a021221f3c48	7777710500.000000	U_SEBAST_AC383055	[Update to MTV 2.11.6]:\nhere is a quick recap of today’s meeting regarding our path forward with the MTV upgrade and resolving our recent migration blockers.\n\nThe next batch of clusters slated for migration are still running VIB 2.0 on the ESXi hosts, which conflicts with the updated MTV version.\nNext Steps:\n\nImmediate Retry: We are going to re-run all the failed migrations from our previous batches on the target clusters that already have VIB 3.0 installed to validate the fixes.\nWeekend Upgrades: Abhishek has requested approval from Vinay, he will escalate to Dennis. Dennis will be upgrading the impacted ESXi hosts to VIB 3.0 over this coming weekend.\nIf the weekend upgrades go as planned, our new batches for next week will be running cleanly on VIB 3.0 hosts, allowing us to fully upgrade to MTV 2.11.6 without issues.	{"ts": "7777710500.000000", "text": "[Update to MTV 2.11.6]:\\nhere is a quick recap of today’s meeting regarding our path forward with the MTV upgrade and resolving our recent migration blockers.\\n\\nThe next batch of clusters slated for migration are still running VIB 2.0 on the ESXi hosts, which conflicts with the updated MTV version.\\nNext Steps:\\n\\nImmediate Retry: We are going to re-run all the failed migrations from our previous batches on the target clusters that already have VIB 3.0 installed to validate the fixes.\\nWeekend Upgrades: Abhishek has requested approval from Vinay, he will escalate to Dennis. Dennis will be upgrading the impacted ESXi hosts to VIB 3.0 over this coming weekend.\\nIf the weekend upgrades go as planned, our new batches for next week will be running cleanly on VIB 3.0 hosts, allowing us to fully upgrade to MTV 2.11.6 without issues.", "type": "message", "user": "U_SEBAST_AC383055"}
4be49d34-e92b-4d3c-9b5d-bc7b0516bff6	601beb55-4fa4-493e-aff1-78d6d7c89ba6	7777710600.000000	U_ALEXAN_D03599F6	[Cluster housekeeping] I'm getting small aneurysms when im looking in the cluster and all the events that are just flooding with failures. So I was thinking, how can we improve this situation on a wider scale with some daily maintenance cleanup activities? Of course, what we want to do first and foremost is to fix the rootcause what leaves the litter aro down the clusters with useless activites and failures that consumes way too much resources in total and clutters any logs.\n\nSo...\n\nAny migration related  items in migration workspaces that are older then 7 days, clean them away\nAny PVC pending older then ... hmm, a bit unsure here.. I wanna say 3 days, but maybe it needs a bit more logic in it\nAny pods(not VMs) that are crashlooping, kill them, scale down deployment or whatever it is\n(edited)	{"ts": "7777710600.000000", "text": "[Cluster housekeeping] I'm getting small aneurysms when im looking in the cluster and all the events that are just flooding with failures. So I was thinking, how can we improve this situation on a wider scale with some daily maintenance cleanup activities? Of course, what we want to do first and foremost is to fix the rootcause what leaves the litter aro down the clusters with useless activites and failures that consumes way too much resources in total and clutters any logs.\\n\\nSo...\\n\\nAny migration related  items in migration workspaces that are older then 7 days, clean them away\\nAny PVC pending older then ... hmm, a bit unsure here.. I wanna say 3 days, but maybe it needs a bit more logic in it\\nAny pods(not VMs) that are crashlooping, kill them, scale down deployment or whatever it is\\n(edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_ALEXAN_D03599F6"}
cedbe8e5-1521-4a9e-ba25-cf54b652bbc2	33e582ab-6be1-45a5-99a2-9e4853c6e420	7777710700.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Migration failures !]\nIf anyone can join the bridge? And verify a) no more VIB issues b) find a patter inf posible for XFS issues?Arpit Tolani  [10:01 AM]\n{Migration}\n\n- name: Patch VM video device to virtio\n  kubernetes.core.k8s:\n    host: "{{ oc_api_url }}"\n    api_key: "{{ oc_token }}"\n    validate_certs: false\n\n    state: patched\n\n    api_version: kubevirt.io/v1\n    kind: VirtualMachine\n\n    name: "{{ vm_name }}"\n\n    definition:\n      spec:\n        template:\n          spec:\n            domain:\n              devices:\n                video:\n                  type: virtio\n\n  when:\n    - vm_os_type | default('') == "Windows"\n\nI was working on a ansible script to patch Windows VM just post migration to get correct Video card driver. This script will go in post_migration_run.yaml in line 295.\nWe already have a task there to identity the OS type.. does it look good?\n\nI have a question? Where do I specify namespace? I cant see that in entire logic.. Do we take care of namespace somewhere else, or its something like all VM in a cluster goes to same NS & we are already in that NS, so no need to define it..	{"ts": "7777710700.000000", "text": "[Migration failures !]\\nIf anyone can join the bridge? And verify a) no more VIB issues b) find a patter inf posible for XFS issues?Arpit Tolani  [10:01 AM]\\n{Migration}\\n\\n- name: Patch VM video device to virtio\\n  kubernetes.core.k8s:\\n    host: \\"{{ oc_api_url }}\\"\\n    api_key: \\"{{ oc_token }}\\"\\n    validate_certs: false\\n\\n    state: patched\\n\\n    api_version: kubevirt.io/v1\\n    kind: VirtualMachine\\n\\n    name: \\"{{ vm_name }}\\"\\n\\n    definition:\\n      spec:\\n        template:\\n          spec:\\n            domain:\\n              devices:\\n                video:\\n                  type: virtio\\n\\n  when:\\n    - vm_os_type | default('') == \\"Windows\\"\\n\\nI was working on a ansible script to patch Windows VM just post migration to get correct Video card driver. This script will go in post_migration_run.yaml in line 295.\\nWe already have a task there to identity the OS type.. does it look good?\\n\\nI have a question? Where do I specify namespace? I cant see that in entire logic.. Do we take care of namespace somewhere else, or its something like all VM in a cluster goes to same NS & we are already in that NS, so no need to define it..", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
2660b090-bdcd-4125-b69c-2f1bff6f0926	606e3f36-0ee7-4e4a-a712-b8b99852e031	7777710800.000000	U_ARPIT_8F539902	[Migration]\nFor TC-13 & 14.\n\nUsecase talks about Migrating Multi Homing VMs from vmware to OCP & talks about testing if the migrated VM wont change the nic names & IP provided to interfaces will be same as before after migraiton.\n\nin Windows Boxes, Specific NIC is mapped to specific adapter using MAC ID. & That adapter is assigned the IP, So chances of migration changing the IP address or swapping goes to zero.\nSame goes with linux that HWADDR is mapped to interface & even in config file & that preserves the IP address.\n\nAll we have to make sure is that we are retaining mac address after migraiton. Local test works perfectly in my env,  How do I prove this test case in cust env?	{"ts": "7777710800.000000", "text": "[Migration]\\nFor TC-13 & 14.\\n\\nUsecase talks about Migrating Multi Homing VMs from vmware to OCP & talks about testing if the migrated VM wont change the nic names & IP provided to interfaces will be same as before after migraiton.\\n\\nin Windows Boxes, Specific NIC is mapped to specific adapter using MAC ID. & That adapter is assigned the IP, So chances of migration changing the IP address or swapping goes to zero.\\nSame goes with linux that HWADDR is mapped to interface & even in config file & that preserves the IP address.\\n\\nAll we have to make sure is that we are retaining mac address after migraiton. Local test works perfectly in my env,  How do I prove this test case in cust env?", "type": "message", "user": "U_ARPIT_8F539902"}
8c6b042b-6b40-49bd-bbee-be6cd9d2f9a8	c7affcc1-05e0-4cfc-a81e-9787ba70421c	7777710900.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[cilium vs ovnk] some more cracks on the cilium capability around cross cluster live migrations came out recently. A little nudge and ovnk may be in the race again. Michael is asking to talk to redhat network engineering around cross cluster CUDNs and NSX bridging. @Nikos @Sam Marland we probably just need Brandon Marlow for that conversation. Is that something you guys can setup?	{"ts": "7777710900.000000", "text": "[cilium vs ovnk] some more cracks on the cilium capability around cross cluster live migrations came out recently. A little nudge and ovnk may be in the race again. Michael is asking to talk to redhat network engineering around cross cluster CUDNs and NSX bridging. @Nikos @Sam Marland we probably just need Brandon Marlow for that conversation. Is that something you guys can setup?", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
072610e5-2491-469e-b923-c088c355a1e3	2ffc2acd-9770-480c-8212-d71b3dc9c4ad	7777717800.000000	U_CARINA_D5667D0C	Team, Anne, Nickos, David and I are in a whole day workshop today. Please shout if you need any of us.	{"ts": "7777717800.000000", "text": "Team, Anne, Nickos, David and I are in a whole day workshop today. Please shout if you need any of us.", "type": "message", "user": "U_CARINA_D5667D0C"}
f235b053-e24a-432d-a4b1-ad9401fda2e3	892aa2a7-f975-49ac-82e3-98cf24abc811	7777711100.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[scaling the migration automation] As the migration issues are debugged, we are getting ready to scale up the number of VMs being migrated. The objective is to reach 350 per active DC per day. Today MIOX creates N migration plans with one VM in each. There is investigation happening for having miox creating plans with multiple VMs (@jfeindis involved in it). However there are a few obstacles for this to happen:\n\nESXi hosts pre-flight checks are sequential (using roles and blocks).\nVM pre-flight and post-flight checks are sequential (using roles and blocks). In this case the ansible playbook call AAP with an API to trigger another ansible playbook. resulting in very slow execution.\nthe current AAP box support a maximum of 50/70 concurrent executions.\n\n\n@Petros is putting together a document to address this issues. In proposed actions will be:\n\nconfigure the AAP box to be able to handle more concurrent job @Nikos is this a point where we should create more synergy with the redhat team that is looking after ansible?\nresolve the ansible calling ansible with simple a playbook reusing another playbook\nrefactor the sequential code to be parallelized by the number of VMs in a plan. This is a job that probably AI could do.\n\n\nI'd like more eyes on this as Ansible is not my domain. Also forgive any in accuracy in the terminology.	{"ts": "7777711100.000000", "text": "[scaling the migration automation] As the migration issues are debugged, we are getting ready to scale up the number of VMs being migrated. The objective is to reach 350 per active DC per day. Today MIOX creates N migration plans with one VM in each. There is investigation happening for having miox creating plans with multiple VMs (@jfeindis involved in it). However there are a few obstacles for this to happen:\\n\\nESXi hosts pre-flight checks are sequential (using roles and blocks).\\nVM pre-flight and post-flight checks are sequential (using roles and blocks). In this case the ansible playbook call AAP with an API to trigger another ansible playbook. resulting in very slow execution.\\nthe current AAP box support a maximum of 50/70 concurrent executions.\\n\\n\\n@Petros is putting together a document to address this issues. In proposed actions will be:\\n\\nconfigure the AAP box to be able to handle more concurrent job @Nikos is this a point where we should create more synergy with the redhat team that is looking after ansible?\\nresolve the ansible calling ansible with simple a playbook reusing another playbook\\nrefactor the sequential code to be parallelized by the number of VMs in a plan. This is a job that probably AI could do.\\n\\n\\nI'd like more eyes on this as Ansible is not my domain. Also forgive any in accuracy in the terminology.", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
c1e18db8-5ab7-41d0-b4d8-fc7478e97db5	e6e5c5b4-a152-42cc-8e8a-869f9afc8654	7777711200.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Support Cases needed for Veritas Clusters Migration & Others]\n@jfeind, @Shebi @Petros can we use 5 min of tomorrows VM Migration Workstream call for me to understand which support cases that are in the path to complete RDM and VCS migrations? So I can understand priorization once the most urgent ones are solved?	{"ts": "7777711200.000000", "text": "[Support Cases needed for Veritas Clusters Migration & Others]\\n@jfeind, @Shebi @Petros can we use 5 min of tomorrows VM Migration Workstream call for me to understand which support cases that are in the path to complete RDM and VCS migrations? So I can understand priorization once the most urgent ones are solved?", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
0778e379-d1da-4a5a-ae68-78e536347471	85dd8cb5-0d2a-49a9-b420-f693e71d12f7	7777711300.000000	U_PHILIP_740C6823	[build - PJ]\n\nPrerana and I are working to tune the oc-mirror jobs. \nThe wrapper script has been updated to use the default values for --parallel-images (4) and --parallel-layers (5) and incredibly, it's running fine now :slightly_smiling_face:. We are seeing (as expected) a significant speed improvement. We have already got run time down to 6hrs and hope to bring it down to <5hrs. \nPrerana has asked that we investigate image mirror failures so I've been making some changes after discussions with her.\nWe have disabled the sync for the falcon-operator (crowdstrike) as it will be very tricky to sync this way given their 30 minute token expiry time.\nThe Infoscale operator, requested a while back has never sync'd correctly and Prerana isn't aware of the requirement for it, so it's also now disabled.\nI've created /etc/containers/registries.d/registry.k8s.io.yaml to disable sig-store checks impacting specific images. The signatures of these images are stored on us-central1-docker.pkg.dev which isn't currently allowed through the proxy. \n\n\nIsovalent call is cancelled today as several members of WWT are unavailable.	{"ts": "7777711300.000000", "text": "[build - PJ]\\n\\nPrerana and I are working to tune the oc-mirror jobs. \\nThe wrapper script has been updated to use the default values for --parallel-images (4) and --parallel-layers (5) and incredibly, it's running fine now :slightly_smiling_face:. We are seeing (as expected) a significant speed improvement. We have already got run time down to 6hrs and hope to bring it down to <5hrs. \\nPrerana has asked that we investigate image mirror failures so I've been making some changes after discussions with her.\\nWe have disabled the sync for the falcon-operator (crowdstrike) as it will be very tricky to sync this way given their 30 minute token expiry time.\\nThe Infoscale operator, requested a while back has never sync'd correctly and Prerana isn't aware of the requirement for it, so it's also now disabled.\\nI've created /etc/containers/registries.d/registry.k8s.io.yaml to disable sig-store checks impacting specific images. The signatures of these images are stored on us-central1-docker.pkg.dev which isn't currently allowed through the proxy. \\n\\n\\nIsovalent call is cancelled today as several members of WWT are unavailable.", "type": "message", "user": "U_PHILIP_740C6823"}
9e95179a-500e-42f4-871d-002862bb1202	b3ef66e3-e3c9-49b0-8b61-9935c300ac31	7777711400.000000	U_CARINA_D5667D0C	[Recharge day cover]\n\n@pdelashe @Shebi @atolani @Anne - all of you said you could swap your days and cover migration on Friday - Recharge day. Is this still the case? please let me know so we can get organised for then :thank_you_smile:	{"ts": "7777711400.000000", "text": "[Recharge day cover]\\n\\n@pdelashe @Shebi @atolani @Anne - all of you said you could swap your days and cover migration on Friday - Recharge day. Is this still the case? please let me know so we can get organised for then :thank_you_smile:", "type": "message", "user": "U_CARINA_D5667D0C"}
15e3dd66-553e-4524-9e1e-9ccdc843dffb	676ca122-67ea-45d6-9a1a-6a1d4ae5558f	7777711500.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Migration update Bundle 08:00 AM]\nWe already have some errors in the migration. Triage is being done (to recognize common errors). I've updated the Proactive suport case with Zoom links for each migration bundle (edited)	{"ts": "7777711500.000000", "text": "[Migration update Bundle 08:00 AM]\\nWe already have some errors in the migration. Triage is being done (to recognize common errors). I've updated the Proactive suport case with Zoom links for each migration bundle (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
c7af76c0-4944-47c4-bc9b-74efe2d700f0	89423714-415e-4027-b5fb-0a258947bcf1	7777711600.000000	U_SEBAST_AC383055	[Migration status 18.05.2026]:\n\nOverall, we had a successful day, processing 113 VMs with an approximate 89% success rate (including those remediated via Fix-Forward/Retry). Bundle 1C was a perfect run with zero rollbacks.\n\n    :large_green_circle: Success (Clean & Fix-Forward/Retry): 101 VMs\n    :red_circle: Rollbacks: 12 VMs\n\nPrimary Blockers & Rollback Reasons:\n\nXFS File System Corruption: Caused 4 rollbacks across Bundles 1A and 1B. As established, this is an OS-level issue on the source VMs that requires remediation prior to migration. We were having support from Red Hat Support troubleshooting one of the rolled back VMs, and we found something interesting. When we ran medatada dump on the XFS filesystems.\n\n\n\n\n\nCgroup / Sysroot Filesystem Mounts: Caused 8 rollbacks in Bundle 1B (e.g., “No file system mounted for cgroup”). (This was related to LUKS encrypted filesystem)\n\nFix-Forwards (Resolved): Mostly related to missing IPs, QEMU/OCP agent issues, intermittent vCenter connectivity, and VMTools failing to uninstall cleanly post-migration. (edited) \nimage.png Arpit Tolani  [10:46 AM]\nany red Hat doc I try to open from VDI gets blocked with error "I do not have general internet access right, if our business unit allows that, we can follow the instructions to request the same"\n\nis it allowed for our team? accessing internet on VDI & Bank Laptop?	{"ts": "7777711600.000000", "text": "[Migration status 18.05.2026]:\\n\\nOverall, we had a successful day, processing 113 VMs with an approximate 89% success rate (including those remediated via Fix-Forward/Retry). Bundle 1C was a perfect run with zero rollbacks.\\n\\n    :large_green_circle: Success (Clean & Fix-Forward/Retry): 101 VMs\\n    :red_circle: Rollbacks: 12 VMs\\n\\nPrimary Blockers & Rollback Reasons:\\n\\nXFS File System Corruption: Caused 4 rollbacks across Bundles 1A and 1B. As established, this is an OS-level issue on the source VMs that requires remediation prior to migration. We were having support from Red Hat Support troubleshooting one of the rolled back VMs, and we found something interesting. When we ran medatada dump on the XFS filesystems.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nCgroup / Sysroot Filesystem Mounts: Caused 8 rollbacks in Bundle 1B (e.g., “No file system mounted for cgroup”). (This was related to LUKS encrypted filesystem)\\n\\nFix-Forwards (Resolved): Mostly related to missing IPs, QEMU/OCP agent issues, intermittent vCenter connectivity, and VMTools failing to uninstall cleanly post-migration. (edited) \\nimage.png Arpit Tolani  [10:46 AM]\\nany red Hat doc I try to open from VDI gets blocked with error \\"I do not have general internet access right, if our business unit allows that, we can follow the instructions to request the same\\"\\n\\nis it allowed for our team? accessing internet on VDI & Bank Laptop?", "type": "message", "user": "U_SEBAST_AC383055"}
c70b5d80-4017-44ff-89db-8ca64da875ff	e698334d-462b-45a7-a34b-dfea6930ec64	7777711700.000000	U_CARINA_D5667D0C	[New joiners, HSBC onboarding]\n\nTeam,\nThis morning I got OTP and ID only for three of you. I'll be sharing those with the other colleagues as I get them :wink:	{"ts": "7777711700.000000", "text": "[New joiners, HSBC onboarding]\\n\\nTeam,\\nThis morning I got OTP and ID only for three of you. I'll be sharing those with the other colleagues as I get them :wink:", "type": "message", "user": "U_CARINA_D5667D0C"}
3a571cb9-e027-48b8-ba6b-fda03e98c56e	27becdec-71c1-41e4-88e9-1edbea1dda4e	7777711800.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Support to Migrations]\nWe had a call this morning to get some feedback on how we support migrations, and we've agreed to try a "person assigned to a migration bundle" strategy for the remaining of this week, to see if it improves our experience and efficiency, considering also our timezones and the fact that we have now another migration event at 8.00 am uk time\nAs we have Red Hat team members in different time zones, we are thinking on dedicating specific people to handle each bundle to optimize our distribution.\n\n\nArpit will be dedicated to the bundle that runs at 8:00 AM UK Time 12:30 PM India time, \nSebastian will be dedicated to the bundle that runs at the 10:00 AM, UK Time \nJens will be dedicated to the bundle that runs at 14:00 UK Time. \n\n\nNew members like Aray and Snehal will be shadowing in whichever bundles they can join.\n\nDetails on how the process works can be found here:  I also added to that doc how to involve ESS if we need additional support\n\nhttps://docs.google.com/document/d/1yVVRat6To3JSWxPKJZ0Kp-jfDSkN1zPUJz6yVnuQ9xo/edit?tab=t.0	{"ts": "7777711800.000000", "text": "[Support to Migrations]\\nWe had a call this morning to get some feedback on how we support migrations, and we've agreed to try a \\"person assigned to a migration bundle\\" strategy for the remaining of this week, to see if it improves our experience and efficiency, considering also our timezones and the fact that we have now another migration event at 8.00 am uk time\\nAs we have Red Hat team members in different time zones, we are thinking on dedicating specific people to handle each bundle to optimize our distribution.\\n\\n\\nArpit will be dedicated to the bundle that runs at 8:00 AM UK Time 12:30 PM India time, \\nSebastian will be dedicated to the bundle that runs at the 10:00 AM, UK Time \\nJens will be dedicated to the bundle that runs at 14:00 UK Time. \\n\\n\\nNew members like Aray and Snehal will be shadowing in whichever bundles they can join.\\n\\nDetails on how the process works can be found here:  I also added to that doc how to involve ESS if we need additional support\\n\\nhttps://docs.google.com/document/d/1yVVRat6To3JSWxPKJZ0Kp-jfDSkN1zPUJz6yVnuQ9xo/edit?tab=t.0", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
740e0c53-52a1-4a20-a5a9-e798d003c3fc	8fd4df94-9acd-4aa8-9c6e-5106f8dfee22	7777717900.000000	U_JANINE_71F80BBD	@Rydekull can you cover the GitOps meeting or do you rather want to join the migration call ?	{"ts": "7777717900.000000", "text": "@Rydekull can you cover the GitOps meeting or do you rather want to join the migration call ?", "type": "message", "user": "U_JANINE_71F80BBD"}
b0ae23ce-52ae-4c67-a741-0c4da98e855c	a1c4951b-4400-4234-88cf-ce2d1cd6b471	7777711900.000000	U_JANINE_71F80BBD	[phase 2 design] @rspazzol @Shebi @Rydekull one question came up regarding how to best utilize different storage arrays in one cluster (non-stretched setup spanning multiple pods). With Pure and px-csi, there seems to be 2 options: one is 1 storage cluster with multiple storage arrays, the storage class or PVC specifying the respective array [1], at provisioning time they need to account for storage capacity to determine which storage class to use. The other would be to utilize CSI topologies [2], woudl still require to determine a storage class at provisioning time.	{"ts": "7777711900.000000", "text": "[phase 2 design] @rspazzol @Shebi @Rydekull one question came up regarding how to best utilize different storage arrays in one cluster (non-stretched setup spanning multiple pods). With Pure and px-csi, there seems to be 2 options: one is 1 storage cluster with multiple storage arrays, the storage class or PVC specifying the respective array [1], at provisioning time they need to account for storage capacity to determine which storage class to use. The other would be to utilize CSI topologies [2], woudl still require to determine a storage class at provisioning time.", "type": "message", "user": "U_JANINE_71F80BBD"}
c4281461-c3e4-4a63-b9b6-19c63ad897f6	bb6fd514-cac5-42db-bb89-45ae40dcb895	7777712000.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[New target clusters for migration]\nAccording to the Migration plan, on May 26th, the VMs will be migrated to 3 different OCP clusters, the ones linked to Fabrics 111, 113 1nd 122.. According to Confluence, 111 is clusters 301,302,303, 113 is clusters 311 and 312, and 122 is cluster 314Raffaele Spazzoli  [4:17 PM]\n[cilium vs ovnk] increasing interest from hsbc on ovnk, two more meetings to be scheduled with engineering: rodamap session, demo session. @Nikos is on it. I also floated the idea that while we are all together in india we could try some of the ovnk features. We will need someone familiar with BGP. @atolani are you by any chance?	{"ts": "7777712000.000000", "text": "[New target clusters for migration]\\nAccording to the Migration plan, on May 26th, the VMs will be migrated to 3 different OCP clusters, the ones linked to Fabrics 111, 113 1nd 122.. According to Confluence, 111 is clusters 301,302,303, 113 is clusters 311 and 312, and 122 is cluster 314Raffaele Spazzoli  [4:17 PM]\\n[cilium vs ovnk] increasing interest from hsbc on ovnk, two more meetings to be scheduled with engineering: rodamap session, demo session. @Nikos is on it. I also floated the idea that while we are all together in india we could try some of the ovnk features. We will need someone familiar with BGP. @atolani are you by any chance?", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
1af3f44e-216d-411b-bd44-db8073b7d671	4f44fad3-e85d-4e63-810a-0b1149c0427a	7777712100.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[unisphere and rawio] dell tested out solution for rawio with their unisphere VM. they are satisfied. hsbc will start the testing now even if the solution is not GA yet. @jfeind this my end up with abhishek wand devdatt and therefore eventually with you.	{"ts": "7777712100.000000", "text": "[unisphere and rawio] dell tested out solution for rawio with their unisphere VM. they are satisfied. hsbc will start the testing now even if the solution is not GA yet. @jfeind this my end up with abhishek wand devdatt and therefore eventually with you.", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
dc1824e8-f246-4793-97c1-2e2741a571fe	dddeeb2a-ce5d-492f-a9e0-1105690d1219	7777712200.000000	U_JAMES_F3856179	[mirroring - cilium]\n@rspazzol you mentioned yesterday if we had the cilium operator locally yet.. I've checked amd looks like we don't. do you want us to add it in now?\n\noc-mirror list operators --catalog=registry.redhat.io/redhat/certified-operator-index:v4.21 --package=cilium-enterprise\n\nNAME               DISPLAY NAME  DEFAULT CHANNEL\ncilium-enterprise                1.16\n\nPACKAGE            CHANNEL  HEAD\ncilium-enterprise  1.16     cilium-enterprise.v1.16.16-xf0717b8@Philip Jones I assume this operator ^ is what we need? can you confirm	{"ts": "7777712200.000000", "text": "[mirroring - cilium]\\n@rspazzol you mentioned yesterday if we had the cilium operator locally yet.. I've checked amd looks like we don't. do you want us to add it in now?\\n\\noc-mirror list operators --catalog=registry.redhat.io/redhat/certified-operator-index:v4.21 --package=cilium-enterprise\\n\\nNAME               DISPLAY NAME  DEFAULT CHANNEL\\ncilium-enterprise                1.16\\n\\nPACKAGE            CHANNEL  HEAD\\ncilium-enterprise  1.16     cilium-enterprise.v1.16.16-xf0717b8@Philip Jones I assume this operator ^ is what we need? can you confirm", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179"}
519cd296-19fd-4b88-bdb0-034c92fbddb5	f31a26b2-8072-42f6-8630-fe716b20f4a3	7777712300.000000	U_SEBAST_AC383055	[Today’s migration update]\nWe pushed through a massive volume today, processing 195 VMs (a mix of 105 RHEL and 89 Windows servers). We achieved a high success rate of approximately 95%, with the vast majority migrating cleanly.\n\n:large_green_circle: Success (Clean): 176 VMs\n:large_yellow_circle: Success (Fix-Forward / Retry): 11 VMs (Primarily IPs not present, agent issues, and disk allocation issues)\n:red_circle: Rollbacks: 8 VMs\n\n\nBlocker Review & Rollback Reasons\nThe 8 rollbacks were entirely driven by two specific issues during the Convert image to kubevirt stage:\n\nXFS Filesystem Corruption / xfs_repair (5 VMs): The underlying filesystem on the source VMs triggered fatal errors during conversion. This is about the Bug I created: https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/MTV-5561 CC @Nick Evans \nOut of Memory (OOM) / setfiles (2 VMs): The conversion appliance ran out of memory, causing the setfiles process to crash. @James Force this is the fix using Gatekeeper cc @rspazzol \n\n\nTomorrow’s Troubleshooting Sync: We have an approved change request to use one of the affected production servers for live debugging. Tomorrow at 11 AM CEST, we will have a joint debugging session with the customer, Red Hat Support, and Engineering to isolate exactly when and where the XFS corruption is being triggered. Anyone is welcome to join, I don’t have the zoom link, but I will share it when I get it.\n:jira: MTV-5561 [New] : virt-v2v fails during migration of RHEL 7 VMs due to XFS filesystem errors, potentially related to RHEL 10.1 xfs_repair running against a RHEL 7 XFS filesystem.Added by assisted-installer-jira-unfurlerCarina Almeida  [9:33 AM]\n:exclamation:[Read me - CONFIDENTIAL] :exclamation:\n\nTeam,\n\nJust a quick but important heads-up regarding current project dynamics.\n\nWe are observing some internal tension on the bank's side right now coming from their Engineering. None of this is directed at Red Hat, but it is impacting meeting atmospheres.\n\nPlease keep these two rules of thumb in mind:\n\nIf bank stakeholders are arguing or there's clear tension, please stay neutral and step back from the debate.\nIf Red Hat is put on the spot or challenged, do not debate it live. Interject politely, ask to take the subject offline, and immediately bring Anne or myself into the conversation.\n\n\nThank you all for maintaining our usual high standards of professionalism. :pray:	{"ts": "7777712300.000000", "text": "[Today’s migration update]\\nWe pushed through a massive volume today, processing 195 VMs (a mix of 105 RHEL and 89 Windows servers). We achieved a high success rate of approximately 95%, with the vast majority migrating cleanly.\\n\\n:large_green_circle: Success (Clean): 176 VMs\\n:large_yellow_circle: Success (Fix-Forward / Retry): 11 VMs (Primarily IPs not present, agent issues, and disk allocation issues)\\n:red_circle: Rollbacks: 8 VMs\\n\\n\\nBlocker Review & Rollback Reasons\\nThe 8 rollbacks were entirely driven by two specific issues during the Convert image to kubevirt stage:\\n\\nXFS Filesystem Corruption / xfs_repair (5 VMs): The underlying filesystem on the source VMs triggered fatal errors during conversion. This is about the Bug I created: https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/MTV-5561 CC @Nick Evans \\nOut of Memory (OOM) / setfiles (2 VMs): The conversion appliance ran out of memory, causing the setfiles process to crash. @James Force this is the fix using Gatekeeper cc @rspazzol \\n\\n\\nTomorrow’s Troubleshooting Sync: We have an approved change request to use one of the affected production servers for live debugging. Tomorrow at 11 AM CEST, we will have a joint debugging session with the customer, Red Hat Support, and Engineering to isolate exactly when and where the XFS corruption is being triggered. Anyone is welcome to join, I don’t have the zoom link, but I will share it when I get it.\\n:jira: MTV-5561 [New] : virt-v2v fails during migration of RHEL 7 VMs due to XFS filesystem errors, potentially related to RHEL 10.1 xfs_repair running against a RHEL 7 XFS filesystem.Added by assisted-installer-jira-unfurlerCarina Almeida  [9:33 AM]\\n:exclamation:[Read me - CONFIDENTIAL] :exclamation:\\n\\nTeam,\\n\\nJust a quick but important heads-up regarding current project dynamics.\\n\\nWe are observing some internal tension on the bank's side right now coming from their Engineering. None of this is directed at Red Hat, but it is impacting meeting atmospheres.\\n\\nPlease keep these two rules of thumb in mind:\\n\\nIf bank stakeholders are arguing or there's clear tension, please stay neutral and step back from the debate.\\nIf Red Hat is put on the spot or challenged, do not debate it live. Interject politely, ask to take the subject offline, and immediately bring Anne or myself into the conversation.\\n\\n\\nThank you all for maintaining our usual high standards of professionalism. :pray:", "type": "message", "user": "U_SEBAST_AC383055"}
ce28a318-a334-4815-9386-34de26fbbb1e	f31a26b2-8072-42f6-8630-fe716b20f4a3	7777712301.000000	U_SEBAST_AC383055	[Today’s Migration update]: Today was a very challenging day on the storage front, but I want to resonate with @Carina Almeida by saying a massive thank you to everyone. Today was one of those days where the true Red Hat spirit shone through. We collaborated across different areas under pressure, maintaining a highly professional and supportive atmosphere.\n\nWe processed a total of 118 VMs across three batches today. Unfortunately, we hit massive storage-layer blockers that forced a high number of rollbacks.\n\n:large_green_circle: Success (Clean): 41 VMs\n:large_yellow_circle: Success (Fix-Forward / Retry): 5 VMs\n:red_circle: Rollbacks: 72 VMs\n:x: Failed: 0 VMs\n\n\nThe high rollback rate today was almost entirely driven by underlying storage infrastructure issues during the Allocate Disks phase:\n\nCopy-Offload ESX HBA UID Errors: A massive chunk of Bundle C failed because copy-offload could not add the ESX HBA UID. \nPowerMax & LUN Mapping: Several Windows VMs failed to allocate disks due to PowerMax clonner initialization failures (“Service Unavailable”) or failing to map LUNs.\n\n\nThe Good News (XFS Resolved!): I spent the majority of today focused on the persistent XFS repair bug. By implementing Option A (the custom virt-v2v image from Engineering that skips the native XFS repair step), we successfully bypassed the conversion crashes. I will make sure this gets into production quickly so we can reduce the number of failures. Tomorrow I will check more on the Dell issue, to see how I can help.	{"ts": "7777712301.000000", "text": "[Today’s Migration update]: Today was a very challenging day on the storage front, but I want to resonate with @Carina Almeida by saying a massive thank you to everyone. Today was one of those days where the true Red Hat spirit shone through. We collaborated across different areas under pressure, maintaining a highly professional and supportive atmosphere.\\n\\nWe processed a total of 118 VMs across three batches today. Unfortunately, we hit massive storage-layer blockers that forced a high number of rollbacks.\\n\\n:large_green_circle: Success (Clean): 41 VMs\\n:large_yellow_circle: Success (Fix-Forward / Retry): 5 VMs\\n:red_circle: Rollbacks: 72 VMs\\n:x: Failed: 0 VMs\\n\\n\\nThe high rollback rate today was almost entirely driven by underlying storage infrastructure issues during the Allocate Disks phase:\\n\\nCopy-Offload ESX HBA UID Errors: A massive chunk of Bundle C failed because copy-offload could not add the ESX HBA UID. \\nPowerMax & LUN Mapping: Several Windows VMs failed to allocate disks due to PowerMax clonner initialization failures (“Service Unavailable”) or failing to map LUNs.\\n\\n\\nThe Good News (XFS Resolved!): I spent the majority of today focused on the persistent XFS repair bug. By implementing Option A (the custom virt-v2v image from Engineering that skips the native XFS repair step), we successfully bypassed the conversion crashes. I will make sure this gets into production quickly so we can reduce the number of failures. Tomorrow I will check more on the Dell issue, to see how I can help.", "type": "message", "user": "U_SEBAST_AC383055", "thread_ts": "7777712300.000000"}
793a6575-e261-4001-8e1a-dcf1b8e89c70	3251777b-6fec-45ae-ba8d-f73732bd2ac1	7777712400.000000	U_SEBAST_AC383055	[XFS Corruption internal meeting]\nEngineering is chasing the XFS repair failures root cause as a kernel mismatch between the RHEL 7 source VMs and the RHEL 10 conversion appliance environment. Because the filesystems are mounted read-only during the conversion, critical on-disk format repairs cannot be completed, causing the process to fail.\n\nDuring our upcoming debug session with the customer, we will test two specific approaches:\n\nOption A (Custom Image): Use a modified virt-v2v image provided by Engineering that explicitly skips the XFS repair step. @Philip Jones I need help to upload this to Nexus, I have it already in my VDI, but not sure how to use it in Nexus to use it in Forklift as this:\noc patch ForkliftController -n openshift-mtv forklift-controller --type=merge -p '{"spec": {"virt_v2v_image_fqin":"quay.io/mnecas0/forklift-virt-v2v:xfs_repair"}}'\n\nOption B (Raw Copy Mode): Use raw copy mode to entirely circumvent the conversion issues (though this will require manual post-migration performance tuning by the customer).\n\nTo capture data in the VM, an ISO is being generated, but I am not sure if we will have enough time to test this.	{"ts": "7777712400.000000", "text": "[XFS Corruption internal meeting]\\nEngineering is chasing the XFS repair failures root cause as a kernel mismatch between the RHEL 7 source VMs and the RHEL 10 conversion appliance environment. Because the filesystems are mounted read-only during the conversion, critical on-disk format repairs cannot be completed, causing the process to fail.\\n\\nDuring our upcoming debug session with the customer, we will test two specific approaches:\\n\\nOption A (Custom Image): Use a modified virt-v2v image provided by Engineering that explicitly skips the XFS repair step. @Philip Jones I need help to upload this to Nexus, I have it already in my VDI, but not sure how to use it in Nexus to use it in Forklift as this:\\noc patch ForkliftController -n openshift-mtv forklift-controller --type=merge -p '{\\"spec\\": {\\"virt_v2v_image_fqin\\":\\"quay.io/mnecas0/forklift-virt-v2v:xfs_repair\\"}}'\\n\\nOption B (Raw Copy Mode): Use raw copy mode to entirely circumvent the conversion issues (though this will require manual post-migration performance tuning by the customer).\\n\\nTo capture data in the VM, an ISO is being generated, but I am not sure if we will have enough time to test this.", "type": "message", "user": "U_SEBAST_AC383055"}
a98a85e0-4bf3-4df4-8642-4f64749efa39	34410998-99f5-4a72-b996-56962e675845	7777712500.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[vm migration planning and node re-purposing] at the strategic meeting it turned out that Accenture is planning migration solely based on the IT service level concept. This means that VMs do not come from the same VMware cluster. As a result now that we are at ~700 migrated VMs we still cannot re-purpose any node. A request will be made to Accenture to consider that further requirement in their planning.	{"ts": "7777712500.000000", "text": "[vm migration planning and node re-purposing] at the strategic meeting it turned out that Accenture is planning migration solely based on the IT service level concept. This means that VMs do not come from the same VMware cluster. As a result now that we are at ~700 migrated VMs we still cannot re-purpose any node. A request will be made to Accenture to consider that further requirement in their planning.", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
73bc0bce-2d35-4282-9fb9-8f1b70a288b3	626c56c3-d2da-46ec-a986-b2dafcdc8fcd	7777712600.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Migrations from and to Dell storage] :alarm_flash:\nFYI, but today migrations were either DELL is the origin or the destination, are failing. The short error is "Copy Offload failed to add the ESX HBA UID. Source Pure Target DELL" I'm not sure on which side it is (Miox, MTV, ???) but it will porbably start to have lots of visibility (edited)	{"ts": "7777712600.000000", "text": "[Migrations from and to Dell storage] :alarm_flash:\\nFYI, but today migrations were either DELL is the origin or the destination, are failing. The short error is \\"Copy Offload failed to add the ESX HBA UID. Source Pure Target DELL\\" I'm not sure on which side it is (Miox, MTV, ???) but it will porbably start to have lots of visibility (edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
cbc0e6bc-a8c5-4e39-8111-95ebb0f7d995	44932932-f15c-4564-a7b0-9ea6b067e4a6	7777712700.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Migration update - Reasons for Failure]\nAfter our last Retro, we decided to capture the reasons for failure during the migrations; these reasons are provided by the customer's SRE team and may / may not be 100% accurate, but as they use now a consistent terminology, it is easier for me to start getting statistics per error type. Added a new Tab to my spreadsheet with that info: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/13zteoVDscXt-TSb0ytvHaXIzFrqnM85K9jSDppxEK1Q/edit?gid=900127641#gid=900127641	{"ts": "7777712700.000000", "text": "[Migration update - Reasons for Failure]\\nAfter our last Retro, we decided to capture the reasons for failure during the migrations; these reasons are provided by the customer's SRE team and may / may not be 100% accurate, but as they use now a consistent terminology, it is easier for me to start getting statistics per error type. Added a new Tab to my spreadsheet with that info: https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/13zteoVDscXt-TSb0ytvHaXIzFrqnM85K9jSDppxEK1Q/edit?gid=900127641#gid=900127641", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
c191be29-8b74-405b-ac8a-8da725202f9d	3165e2cc-2af5-4873-95f8-30c693769bd4	7777712800.000000	U_GOOGLE_64E3F7FD	[info] Regarding https://access.redhat.com/support/cases/#/case/04426565, I've asked support if they can confirm which version this bug (pvc / pv uid mismatch) was introduced in. We may be better off reverting to a prior version until the fix is available.	{"ts": "7777712800.000000", "text": "[info] Regarding https://access.redhat.com/support/cases/#/case/04426565, I've asked support if they can confirm which version this bug (pvc / pv uid mismatch) was introduced in. We may be better off reverting to a prior version until the fix is available.", "type": "message", "user": "U_GOOGLE_64E3F7FD"}
38ba4076-ab87-4a7d-ab3f-c2c4c0839d9d	f378b82b-830c-4d30-be0e-4c47111ca8bc	7777712900.000000	U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E	[KDD] reporting some KDD decisions taken today (for what they are worth)\n\nHIC clusters will be 24 nodes when non-stretched,  48 nodes when stretched. still confusing to me if we actually need stretched  clusters.\nwe will continue with the two namespace approach\nwe will not implement the node firewall operator for now, it needs to be deeply tested.\n(edited)	{"ts": "7777712900.000000", "text": "[KDD] reporting some KDD decisions taken today (for what they are worth)\\n\\nHIC clusters will be 24 nodes when non-stretched,  48 nodes when stretched. still confusing to me if we actually need stretched  clusters.\\nwe will continue with the two namespace approach\\nwe will not implement the node firewall operator for now, it needs to be deeply tested.\\n(edited)", "type": "message", "user": "U_RAFFAE_9DC03E2E"}
4c4ed28d-d962-40ce-916f-50a27561a615	b4197546-f990-4083-aa54-f002aa89663d	7777713000.000000	U_JOHANN_669B3727	[Time Card Submission]\nHi team, as most of you will be out for the quarterly Recharge Day tomorrow & Monday is a bank holiday both in the UK & Germany, please ensure that you log your Time Sheets on Clarity for the bank today and log your Red Hat time cards within the next two hours, so they can be approved on time. Thank you everyone! :sunny:	{"ts": "7777713000.000000", "text": "[Time Card Submission]\\nHi team, as most of you will be out for the quarterly Recharge Day tomorrow & Monday is a bank holiday both in the UK & Germany, please ensure that you log your Time Sheets on Clarity for the bank today and log your Red Hat time cards within the next two hours, so they can be approved on time. Thank you everyone! :sunny:", "type": "message", "user": "U_JOHANN_669B3727"}
c6f0baff-4fa5-495c-af66-ba1527434dc9	ff276b96-0418-42fa-ba50-123438d87d88	7777713100.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Migration Bundle Handover]\nAfter a short retro today, we have agreed that we need an easy way to handover each bundle migration status to the following team, for background and context and to prevent troubleshooting the same issues twice. I've drafted a "Tab" in this channel rather than creating a separate channel or document, so each team can provide their inputs onthe the bundle once its is completed. Please let me know what do you think of the format and if it is useful. https://redhat.enterprise.slack.com/docs/T027F3GAJ/F0B4VJB90ET\nCanvas Migration Bundle Handover\nCanvasNote: We don't need the number of success / failures of each migration as those are tracked here:  \nhttps://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/13zteoVDscXt-TSb0ytvHaXIzFrqnM85K9jSDppxEK1Q/edit?gid=0#gid=0\n26 May 2026 - Bucket 4 - Bundle 1c\nObserved Errors:\nPriority Action Items:\nCustomer Mood: \nSupport cases opened:\nRoot Cause Analysis:\n26 May 2026 - Bucket 4 - Bundle 1b\nObserved Errors:	{"ts": "7777713100.000000", "text": "[Migration Bundle Handover]\\nAfter a short retro today, we have agreed that we need an easy way to handover each bundle migration status to the following team, for background and context and to prevent troubleshooting the same issues twice. I've drafted a \\"Tab\\" in this channel rather than creating a separate channel or document, so each team can provide their inputs onthe the bundle once its is completed. Please let me know what do you think of the format and if it is useful. https://redhat.enterprise.slack.com/docs/T027F3GAJ/F0B4VJB90ET\\nCanvas Migration Bundle Handover\\nCanvasNote: We don't need the number of success / failures of each migration as those are tracked here:  \\nhttps://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/13zteoVDscXt-TSb0ytvHaXIzFrqnM85K9jSDppxEK1Q/edit?gid=0#gid=0\\n26 May 2026 - Bucket 4 - Bundle 1c\\nObserved Errors:\\nPriority Action Items:\\nCustomer Mood: \\nSupport cases opened:\\nRoot Cause Analysis:\\n26 May 2026 - Bucket 4 - Bundle 1b\\nObserved Errors:", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
d96eae42-f201-4e37-8c71-54522da2d090	bccaf621-7621-44f8-a6b8-a4cf3731c152	7777715100.000000	U_PHILIP_740C6823	PJ Build Update:\n\nPortworx CSI driver updated to 26.1.1 on all lab clusters.\nLargely successful, but 102 cluster has a MAJOR issue with snapshots... There are >4700 vm snapshots and volumesnapshotcontent resources present, but there are 0 volumesnapshots! This problem is breaking the CSI migration pods and causing them to fail :(\nWe don't have an easy solution or explanation. If we manually create a snapshot, no new snapshot instance is created, just the content and the virtmachine upgrades.	{"ts": "7777715100.000000", "text": "PJ Build Update:\\n\\nPortworx CSI driver updated to 26.1.1 on all lab clusters.\\nLargely successful, but 102 cluster has a MAJOR issue with snapshots... There are >4700 vm snapshots and volumesnapshotcontent resources present, but there are 0 volumesnapshots! This problem is breaking the CSI migration pods and causing them to fail :(\\nWe don't have an easy solution or explanation. If we manually create a snapshot, no new snapshot instance is created, just the content and the virtmachine upgrades.", "type": "message", "user": "U_PHILIP_740C6823"}
7db68359-7dfd-43f7-9aad-f8f280814cb9	c19e517e-d4fb-4f2a-a933-216f0e505d82	7777713200.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Migration Completed]\nAs all DELL VMs were removed from today's migrations, we migrated 9 VMs today, and 2 of them were rolled back due to XFS Issues.Raffaele Spazzoli  [3:23 PM]\n[dell migration failures and CSM observability module] I'm getting only partial information but it looks like Dell has indicated that the Unisphere API  was overwhelmed because of the CSM observability module making to many requests. The CSM observability module is now disabled in prod. The observability team was really sorry for that. I tried to reassure that they didn't do anything wrong they had no easy way to know that they were DOSing the Unisphere API (that metric is not reported and can be seen only from the array GUI; ah the irony!!) . @pdelashe @Shebi @jfeind does this correspond with what you are seeing?	{"ts": "7777713200.000000", "text": "[Migration Completed]\\nAs all DELL VMs were removed from today's migrations, we migrated 9 VMs today, and 2 of them were rolled back due to XFS Issues.Raffaele Spazzoli  [3:23 PM]\\n[dell migration failures and CSM observability module] I'm getting only partial information but it looks like Dell has indicated that the Unisphere API  was overwhelmed because of the CSM observability module making to many requests. The CSM observability module is now disabled in prod. The observability team was really sorry for that. I tried to reassure that they didn't do anything wrong they had no easy way to know that they were DOSing the Unisphere API (that metric is not reported and can be seen only from the array GUI; ah the irony!!) . @pdelashe @Shebi @jfeind does this correspond with what you are seeing?", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
2cf32401-4136-4a3b-83e2-a7d0528ddcc9	1f4c3844-59e5-4dc5-b983-897e2b448f2a	7777713300.000000	U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB	[Migration Today]\nAs Ann said, VMs have been organized to remove those sitting on DELL so the plan for today is 3 budles, one at 10.00 and another 2 at 13.00. Arpit and Snehal will be supporting the first one, and Sebastian and anyone else working today, will be supporting the 13.00. Let's hope they go smoother than yesterday'simage.png Patricia de las Heras Granaglia  [9:41 AM]\n@Shebi can you share with me the XFS rollout plan? I was not able to keep track of all messages. What needs to be done and when will it be done. Tnx!	{"ts": "7777713300.000000", "text": "[Migration Today]\\nAs Ann said, VMs have been organized to remove those sitting on DELL so the plan for today is 3 budles, one at 10.00 and another 2 at 13.00. Arpit and Snehal will be supporting the first one, and Sebastian and anyone else working today, will be supporting the 13.00. Let's hope they go smoother than yesterday'simage.png Patricia de las Heras Granaglia  [9:41 AM]\\n@Shebi can you share with me the XFS rollout plan? I was not able to keep track of all messages. What needs to be done and when will it be done. Tnx!", "type": "message", "user": "U_PATRIC_80EFC8EB"}
a0b23755-28c0-4301-9f18-8414662e53cc	3a2ae747-2657-4e4c-a7a7-3b9d03e7c75c	7777713400.000000	U_JAMES_F3856179	[security - network policies] \n@Rydekull one for next week...(FYI i'm not actuallly working today, just so happen to have a bit of overlap with some uni work of mine... hence the question :wink:)\n\nAs for the workload networkpolicy he wants setting up is that just a deny all basically? - I was thinking... even tho we're using CUDN we still need to account for live migration and maybe namespace to namespace migration right (forward looking a bit)? as those comms would be over the pod network right? I think I'll maybe just need to test this in a lab, but curious if you have any pointers here. How deny all are we going for here? Can you remind me on the logic of what he wants to do here and why (if its more than just because) - something re system level workloads right? I was just thinking of what we need to consider here if anything at all\n\nI assume Natti is aware of this yeah? (i'm sure we've talked on this before) https://access.redhat.com/articles/7133113 + https://docs.google.com/document/d/1m1ne1z4iBfB66d30-HXUN9-FDBGtXN75asVe5oyWxiA/edit?usp=sharing just wanted to check on that too\nRed Hat Customer PortalOperators shipping with network policies may require OCP cluster upgrade before they can be upgraded - Red Hat Customer PortalBeginning February 2027, each Operator from Red Hat is required to ship with Network Policies. This will require to update your cluster to a new minimum versions, otherwise these updates will fail to apply and manual intervention is required.Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga  [5:03 PM]\n[Migration Update]\nThe Situarday with Dell, there was a clear directive from Narender to keep all VMs residing on Dell storage out of today’s migration batches until further notice. Unfortunately, there was a breakdown in coordination with Accenture, and they were unable to triage and filter the Dell VMs out of the environment. As a result, we spent the whole day in firefighting mode.\n\nWe used the failed runs to gather additional logs from the system. These logs have been handed over to Dell so they can finally provide a permanent fix for the storage allocation.\n\nThe Good News (XFS Resolved!):\nWe successfully pushed the fix for the XFS repair :party_cat: The few RHEL migrations that did succeed today (on non-Dell storage) proved that the XFS workaround is fully functional. This clears one of the blockers for our RHEL 7 workloads moving forward.\n\nNext Steps & Tuesday Resumption:\nGiven the high volume of Dell-related failures, Vinay made the right call to cancel the remaining migrations for today. We are going to regroup and resume migrations on Tuesday (Hoping ACN does a good triage).\n\nMigration Statistics (May 22, 2026)\nWe had roughly 122 VMs in scope today across the batches.\n    :large_green_circle: Success (Clean & Fix/Retry): 9 VMs\n    :red_circle: Rollbacks: 59 VMs (Almost exclusively Dell Storage / LUN Mapping / Allocate Disk failures)\n    :white_circle: Cancelled: 54 VMs (Cancelled by Vinay’s directive to halt the Dell batches)\n\nHave a great weekend everyone, and we will pick this back up on Tuesday!	{"ts": "7777713400.000000", "text": "[security - network policies] \\n@Rydekull one for next week...(FYI i'm not actuallly working today, just so happen to have a bit of overlap with some uni work of mine... hence the question :wink:)\\n\\nAs for the workload networkpolicy he wants setting up is that just a deny all basically? - I was thinking... even tho we're using CUDN we still need to account for live migration and maybe namespace to namespace migration right (forward looking a bit)? as those comms would be over the pod network right? I think I'll maybe just need to test this in a lab, but curious if you have any pointers here. How deny all are we going for here? Can you remind me on the logic of what he wants to do here and why (if its more than just because) - something re system level workloads right? I was just thinking of what we need to consider here if anything at all\\n\\nI assume Natti is aware of this yeah? (i'm sure we've talked on this before) https://access.redhat.com/articles/7133113 + https://docs.google.com/document/d/1m1ne1z4iBfB66d30-HXUN9-FDBGtXN75asVe5oyWxiA/edit?usp=sharing just wanted to check on that too\\nRed Hat Customer PortalOperators shipping with network policies may require OCP cluster upgrade before they can be upgraded - Red Hat Customer PortalBeginning February 2027, each Operator from Red Hat is required to ship with Network Policies. This will require to update your cluster to a new minimum versions, otherwise these updates will fail to apply and manual intervention is required.Sebastian Rodriguez Isaziga  [5:03 PM]\\n[Migration Update]\\nThe Situarday with Dell, there was a clear directive from Narender to keep all VMs residing on Dell storage out of today’s migration batches until further notice. Unfortunately, there was a breakdown in coordination with Accenture, and they were unable to triage and filter the Dell VMs out of the environment. As a result, we spent the whole day in firefighting mode.\\n\\nWe used the failed runs to gather additional logs from the system. These logs have been handed over to Dell so they can finally provide a permanent fix for the storage allocation.\\n\\nThe Good News (XFS Resolved!):\\nWe successfully pushed the fix for the XFS repair :party_cat: The few RHEL migrations that did succeed today (on non-Dell storage) proved that the XFS workaround is fully functional. This clears one of the blockers for our RHEL 7 workloads moving forward.\\n\\nNext Steps & Tuesday Resumption:\\nGiven the high volume of Dell-related failures, Vinay made the right call to cancel the remaining migrations for today. We are going to regroup and resume migrations on Tuesday (Hoping ACN does a good triage).\\n\\nMigration Statistics (May 22, 2026)\\nWe had roughly 122 VMs in scope today across the batches.\\n    :large_green_circle: Success (Clean & Fix/Retry): 9 VMs\\n    :red_circle: Rollbacks: 59 VMs (Almost exclusively Dell Storage / LUN Mapping / Allocate Disk failures)\\n    :white_circle: Cancelled: 54 VMs (Cancelled by Vinay’s directive to halt the Dell batches)\\n\\nHave a great weekend everyone, and we will pick this back up on Tuesday!", "type": "message", "user": "U_JAMES_F3856179"}
